\title{Singleton-Square Bounds for the\\
Nine-Dimensional Snake-in-the-Box Problem}
\author{Iori Hironaka \and Tommy Ma \and Steven Zhan}
\date{\today}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
Let $a(9)$ be the largest number of edges in an induced path in the nine-dimensional binary hypercube. An explicit construction and a completed contributor proof give $191\le a(9)\le235$. We present the square census and endpoint argument yielding $39m+2s_0\le9200$, where $m$ is the path length and $s_0$ counts empty coordinate squares. We then retain empty squares through integer shares. Vertices forced absent by a selected segment give share lower bounds; exhaustive local neighbour choices and saturation of singleton-count caps strengthen them. Three short geometric arguments each force at least three singleton squares at a specified internal vertex. These results apply to arbitrary matching snakes and have passed Lean checks. Explicit overlap and boundary accounting explains how local shares can enter a global upper bound. In the complete recorded exploratory model, the new restrictions raise the exact integer minimum from $9891/20$ to $18823/38$. An attaining cycle and an all-edge potential certify the finite computation, but inherited numerical premises and the complete graph-to-snake assembly remain unfinished. The remaining minimum-mean family has 70 states and 99 transitions. Earlier local inequalities, sharpness witnesses, and finite-model obstructions are retained with their scopes. The value 196 remains a numerical conjecture, without a matching construction or optimality proof.
\end{abstract}
\clearpage
\setcounter{tocdepth}{1}
\tableofcontents
\clearpage

\section{Problem, notation, and principal result}\label{sec:problem}
\begin{definition}
The graph $\Q_n$ has vertex set $\{0,1\}^n$, with adjacency $u\sim v$ when $\Ham(u,v)=1$. A snake of length $L$ is a sequence of distinct vertices $v_0,\ldots,v_L$ such that
\[
v_i\sim v_j\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad |i-j|=1\qquad(i\ne j).
\]
Length counts edges. Thus a length-$L$ snake has $L+1$ vertices. Let $a(n)$ denote the largest possible length.
\end{definition}
Finiteness of $\Q_n$ ensures that a maximum exists. A transition sequence $t_1\cdots t_L$, with $0\le t_i<n$, specifies a path from the zero vertex by
\[
v_0=0,\qquad v_i=v_{i-1}\mathbin{\oplus}2^{t_i}.
\]
Integers encode binary words; $\oplus$ is exclusive-or. Translation by exclusive-or with a fixed word, coordinate permutations, and reversal preserve the snake conditions.

The exact values through dimension eight are listed in OEIS A099155 \cite{oeis}. Carlson and Hougen constructed a length-98 snake in dimension eight; the upper bound was established by exhaustive search by Östergård and Pettersson \cite{ostergard}. The universal bound $a(8)=98$ is used as a cited result here; a witness of that length is reproduced in Appendix~\ref{app:witness}, while the exhaustive upper bound is taken from that reference.

Orland et al. supply 191-edge snakes in dimension nine and a public collection of 131 inequivalent examples \cite{orland,data}. Optimality is unresolved. These examples are not known to exhaust all isometry classes of longest snakes. The recorded examples are blocked at their endpoints. Section~\ref{sec:optimality} explains why this does not establish optimality.

\subsection{Counting notation}
For a snake $P=(x_0,\ldots,x_m)$, write $S=V(P)$ and $v=|S|=m+1$. Thus $m$ always counts edges and $v$ counts vertices. A coordinate square is a two-dimensional face of the cube. Let $s_k$ count the distinct coordinate squares containing exactly $k$ vertices of $S$. For $x\in S$, let $\alpha_S(x)$ count squares meeting $S$ only at $x$, and put
\[
\alpha_i=\alpha_S(x_i),\qquad T=\sum_{i=0}^{m}\alpha_i=s_1.
\]
Whenever a statement uses both endpoints, assume $m\ge1$. The single-vertex path is handled separately in each global bound.
For an internal vertex define
\[
b_i=\min\{6,\alpha_i\},\qquad \delta_i=\alpha_i-b_i.
\]
Thus $\alpha_i=b_i+\delta_i$ with $\delta_i\ge0$. A count symbol $b_i<6$ specifies the exact count; $b_i=6$ represents every count from six through twenty-one. Lemma~\ref{lem:window-missing-corners} explains the upper value twenty-one. When these quantities are summed over the whole path, the same definitions are used at the endpoints, without asserting the internal upper bound there.

\subsection{Results and proof dependencies}
\begin{theorem}[Main result]\label{thm:main}
The maximum edge length of an induced path in $\Q_9$ satisfies
\[
\boxed{191\le a(9)\le235.}
\]
\end{theorem}
The lower bound is verified from the explicit transition sequence in Theorem~\ref{thm:lower}. The proof of the upper bound has three steps. First, count coordinate squares exactly and establish positive singleton counts, with a stronger contribution at each endpoint. Second, exclude a finite family of local configurations and verify an integer potential on every remaining transition. Third, sum the potential inequalities and substitute the resulting count estimate into the square identity. Section~\ref{sec:upper-proof} gives this argument directly for 235.

The subsequent argument develops the empty-square term in three stages. Sections~\ref{sec:empty-packing} and~\ref{sec:forced-charge} define integer shares and prove lower bounds from absent vertices, finite alternatives, and singleton-count saturation. Section~\ref{sec:long-counts} gives direct geometric restrictions on longer segments. Sections~\ref{sec:common-budget} and~\ref{sec:long-models} account for overlapping charges and all boundary losses, then separate complete finite models from restricted critical families. The numerical conjecture and construction criteria are assessed after these deductions. Earlier finite-model obstructions and complete witness sequences remain in the appendices. In particular, an exclusion of a minimum-mean cycle is used only within its stated graph; replacement cycles are checked separately.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{p{0.27\textwidth}p{0.62\textwidth}}
\toprule
Claim & Evidence and scope\\\midrule
$a(9)\ge191$ & An explicit snake with every vertex pair checked.\\
$a(9)\le235$ & Geometric window reduction, 1287 catalogue keys, a complete potential check, and the endpoint summation.\\
Pair bounds seven and eight & Complete Lean checks, explicit sharpness witnesses, and an endpoint-distance criterion.\\
Local empty-square bounds & Arbitrary-snake proofs from forced absence, neighbour choices and count saturation.\\
Finite-model mean $18823/4560$ & A cycle and an all-edge potential; inherited premises and complete snake coverage remain unfinished.\\
