\begin{theorem}[Verified lower bound]\label{thm:lower}
A 191-edge snake exists in $\Q_9$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The first transition sequence in Appendix~\ref{app:witness} is the published Appendix E example of \cite{orland}. Starting at zero reconstructs 192 vertices. For each $0\le i<j\le191$, direct calculation gives $v_i\ne v_j$ and $\Ham(v_i,v_j)=1$ exactly when $j=i+1$. This is a finite verification of $\binom{192}{2}=18\,336$ unordered pairs using the displayed transition sequence, and establishes a snake of length 191.
\end{proof}

\section{The exact square census}\label{sec:incidence}
\begin{theorem}[Exact square identity]\label{thm:exact-square}
For every nontrivial snake in $\Q_9$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:exact-square}
T+2s_0+35v=9214.
\end{equation}
In particular, $T+35v\le9214$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The cube has $\binom92 2^7=4608$ coordinate squares, and each vertex belongs to $\binom92=36$ of them. No square has four selected vertices, since these would form a cycle in the induced path. Counting squares and then incidences between selected vertices and squares gives
\[
s_0+s_1+s_2+s_3=4608,\qquad s_1+2s_2+3s_3=36v.
\]
A square with three selected vertices has a unique selected corner adjacent to the other two. That corner is an internal path vertex. Conversely, the two neighbors of each internal path vertex determine a unique coordinate square with three selected vertices; its fourth corner must be absent. These two assignments are inverse, so $s_3=v-2$. Subtracting twice the first census equation from the second yields
\[
-2s_0-s_1+s_3=36v-9216.
\]
Substitute $s_1=T$ and $s_3=v-2$ and rearrange. The inequality follows from $s_0\ge0$.
\end{proof}

The identity states exactly what is lost when empty squares are discarded. A stronger lower estimate for $T$ improves the bound only to the extent allowed by the remaining square census. Section~\ref{sec:empty-packing} assigns some of the term $2s_0$ to selected vertices and retains it in the same argument.

\subsection{Positive singleton counts and endpoint contributions}
The lower estimates used in the global proof require the induced-path condition, including its restriction to two endpoints. The following proofs give these estimates directly. In coordinate expressions such as $ij$ or $aij$, juxtaposition means the sum of the corresponding distinct unit vectors in $\mathbb F_2^9$.

\begin{lemma}[At least one singleton square]\label{lem:one-square}
If $S$ is the vertex set of an induced path in $\Q_9$, then $\alpha_S(x)\ge1$ for every $x\in S$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Translate $x$ to zero. Translation preserves adjacency and the induced-path property. Addition below is coordinatewise modulo two, and $e_i$ denotes the unit vector in coordinate $i$.

First suppose that the path has at most two edges. The three coordinate squares using the pairs $(0,1)$, $(2,3)$, and $(4,5)$ have mutually disjoint sets of nonzero vertices. If none were a singleton square, they would contribute three distinct selected vertices besides zero, exceeding the number of vertices in the path. Thus at least one is a singleton square.

Now suppose that the path has at least three edges, and assume for contradiction that no singleton square exists at zero. For any two distinct coordinates $i,j$ whose unit vectors are absent, the opposite vertex $e_i+e_j$ must then be selected.

Every vertex of a path with at least three edges has an internal path vertex as a neighbor. Choose such a neighbor $e_a$ of zero, and let $c$ be its other path neighbor. Write $c=e_a+e_i$, where $i\ne a$ because $c\ne0$. The unit vector $e_i$ is absent: otherwise $0,e_a,c,e_i,0$ would be a selected four-cycle, which an induced path cannot contain.

At most two unit vectors are selected, since zero has induced degree at most two. Choose a coordinate $b\notin\{a,i\}$ so that every selected unit vector is indexed by $a$ or $b$. If there is a second selected unit vector, take its coordinate; otherwise any coordinate outside $\{a,i\}$ suffices. Set
\[
U=\{0,\ldots,8\}\setminus\{a,b\}.
\]
Then $i\in U$, every unit vector indexed by $U$ is absent, and every pair vertex $e_j+e_k$ with distinct $j,k\in U$ is selected. Every triple vertex $e_j+e_k+e_l$ with distinct $j,k,l\in U$ is absent, because its three pair-vertex neighbors are selected and a path has maximum degree two.

Choose four distinct coordinates $j\in U\setminus\{i\}$; six are available. For each of these four coordinates, define
\[
p_j=e_i+e_j,\qquad q_j=e_a+e_i+e_j.
\]
All four $p_j$ are selected. The four $q_j$ are distinct neighbors of $c$, distinct also from its already selected neighbor $e_a$. Therefore at most one $q_j$ is selected, and at least three are absent.

For each absent $q_j$, the corresponding $p_j$ has at most one selected neighbor. Flipping coordinate $i$ or $j$ gives an absent unit vector; flipping any other coordinate in $U$ gives an excluded triple; and flipping $a$ gives the absent $q_j$. Only the neighbor $e_b+e_i+e_j$ can remain selected. Hence at least three distinct selected vertices have induced degree at most one. In a path with at least three edges, precisely the two endpoints have degree at most one. This contradiction proves the lemma.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[At least four singleton squares at an endpoint]\label{thm:endpoint-four}
If $x$ is an endpoint of a snake in $\Q_9$ having at least one edge, then $\alpha_S(x)\ge4$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Translate $x$ to zero and let $a$ be its sole selected unit neighbor. Suppose, for contradiction, that its family $F$ of missing coordinate pairs has at most three members. Every member of $F$ is a two-element subset of the eight coordinates other than $a$. For distinct $i,j\ne a$, the pair vertex $ij$ is selected if and only if $\{i,j\}\notin F$.

Call a coordinate isolated if it is different from $a$ and occurs in no member of $F$. The members of $F$ cover at most six coordinates, so there are at least two isolated coordinates. If $u,v$ are distinct isolated coordinates, then $uv$ is selected and has at most one selected neighbor, namely $auv$. Its unit neighbors are absent. For every other coordinate $k\notin\{a,u,v\}$, the three vertices $uv,uk,vk$ are selected, so $uvk$ is absent. Thus $uv$ is an endpoint.

There cannot be three isolated coordinates $u,v,w$: the three distinct vertices $0,uv,uw$ would all be endpoints. Consequently there are exactly two isolated coordinates, say $u,v$. The other six coordinates, apart from $a$, must be covered by the at most three missing pairs. They are therefore covered by exactly three pairwise disjoint pairs. In particular, each coordinate $t\notin\{a,u,v\}$ has a unique partner $j$, with $\{t,j\}\in F$ and $j\notin\{a,u,v,t\}$.

The selected vertices $0$ and $uv$ already account for the only two possible endpoints. Fix any $t\notin\{a,u,v\}$ and let $j$ be its partner. The selected pair $tu$ differs from both endpoints and is therefore internal. Its only possible selected neighbors are $atu$ and $tju$: for a third coordinate $k\notin\{a,t,u,j\}$, all three pair vertices $tu,tk,uk$ are selected, making $tuk$ absent. Both $atu,tju$ must therefore be selected. Applying the same argument to $tv$ forces $atv$ to be selected.

It follows that $at$ is absent for every $t\notin\{a,u,v\}$, since otherwise it would have the three distinct selected neighbors $a,atu,atv$. Choose two distinct coordinates $r,s$ outside $\{a,u,v\}$. The preceding argument also gives the selected triples $aru,asu$, so $au$ is absent because its three neighbors $a,aru,asu$ would be selected. Likewise $av$ is absent, using $arv,asv$.

All pair vertices $at$ with $t\ne a$ are now absent. Thus the selected vertex $a$ has only the selected neighbor zero and is itself an endpoint. The vertices $0,uv,a$ are distinct, giving a third endpoint and the required contradiction.
\end{proof}


The endpoint estimate does not extend as a lower bound of two to every internal vertex. Theorem~\ref{thm:square-counterexample} gives a 137-edge snake with an internal count equal to one. The explicit witness and its verification are retained in the appendices.

\subsection{Reading a singleton count from missing corners}
\begin{lemma}[Missing corners at a prescribed internal vertex]\label{lem:window-missing-corners}
Let $S$ be the vertex set of a snake, and suppose $v,v+e_a,v+e_b\in S$, with $a\ne b$. Among the 21 unordered coordinate pairs $\{c,d\}$ disjoint from $\{a,b\}$, exactly $\alpha_S(v)$ have $v+e_c+e_d\notin S$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The two specified neighbors exhaust the selected neighbors of $v$, since a vertex of an induced path has degree at most two. A coordinate square using $a$ or $b$ therefore contains another selected vertex and is not a singleton square at $v$. For a pair avoiding $a$ and $b$, both adjacent corners $v+e_c$ and $v+e_d$ are absent. The square is a singleton precisely when its opposite corner is absent. There are $\binom{7}{2}=21$ such pairs, and every singleton square is counted once.
\end{proof}

\section{The upper bound 235 and the retained empty-square term}\label{sec:upper-proof}
The square identity reduces the upper-bound problem to a lower estimate for $T$. We obtain that estimate from necessary conditions on short path segments. The local conditions exclude configurations, the finite potential combines the exclusions, and the final summation accounts for the ends of the path.
\subsection{Normalization and necessary local conditions}
A finite transition word is normalized by replacing its distinct coordinate labels, in order of first appearance, by $0,1,2,\ldots$. This replacement extends to a permutation of all nine coordinates. Translation makes its first vertex zero. Both operations preserve adjacency, endpoints, and singleton counts. Reversal permits the same exclusion to be used in either orientation.

For any consecutive block of transitions, the difference between its two end vertices is the sum of the corresponding unit vectors in $\mathbb F_2^9$. The two vertices coincide exactly when every coordinate occurs an even number of times. They are adjacent exactly when one coordinate occurs an odd number of times. These parity tests therefore determine whether a normalized word represents an induced segment: no nonempty block may have zero difference, and a block of length greater than one may not have difference of weight one.

Choose two to six consecutive internal centres and include a Hamming neighborhood containing all corners required to count their singleton squares. A hypothetical snake assigns a selected or unselected value to each represented cube vertex. The following restrictions are necessary:
\begin{enumerate}
\item The prescribed segment vertices are selected. A selected vertex has at most two selected neighbors.
\item Wherever all nine neighbors are represented, a selected vertex has degree one or two. Degree-one vertices in this interior are endpoints of the full snake, so there are at most two.
\item No nonempty set of represented vertices inducing minimum degree at least two is entirely selected.
\item The numbers of missing eligible opposite corners agree with the prescribed singleton counts, by Lemma~\ref{lem:window-missing-corners}.
\end{enumerate}
Boundary vertices do not receive an unjustified degree lower bound: some of their neighbors may lie outside the neighborhood. The conditions are necessary for a full snake; a satisfying local assignment need not extend to one.

\subsection{Acyclicity as a finite restriction}
\begin{lemma}[A finite subset has a vertex of degree at most one]\label{lem:subset-low-degree}
Let $P=(p_0,\ldots,p_m)$ be an induced path and let $A$ be a nonempty subset of its vertex set. Some vertex of $A$ has at most one neighbor in $A$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Choose the least index $i$ for which $p_i\in A$. Any neighbor $p_j\in A$ of $p_i$ satisfies $j\ge i$ by minimality and $|j-i|=1$ by the induced-path condition. Thus $j=i+1$. There is at most one such vertex.
\end{proof}
Consequently, a finite nonempty vertex set having two distinct neighbors within the set at every vertex cannot be contained in an induced path. This excludes cycles of arbitrary length and also applies to disconnected unions of cycles. The lemma justifies every finite cycle exclusion used below.


\subsection{The six-centre catalogue and potential}\label{sec:six-centre-235}
Let $\mathcal F_6$ be the completed catalogue of 1287 normalized direction-and-count keys in the contributor archive~\cite{bound235archive}. A key prescribes between two and six consecutive internal counts. As before, a symbol six denotes the interval $[6,21]$. A state consists of a normalized induced seven-direction word and the last five internal count symbols. There are $34\cdot6^5=264384$ states. A transition appends one direction and one count, discards the oldest entries, and renormalizes the directions. It is permitted if the resulting eight-direction word is induced and the new windows avoid the catalogue in both orientations.

\begin{lemma}[Six-centre exclusions and potential]\label{lem:six-centre-235}
Every snake avoids $\mathcal F_6$. There is an integer potential $\Psi$ on the stated finite state set, with $0\le\Psi\le7$, such that every permitted transition $u\to w$ with appended count $b$ satisfies
\begin{equation}\label{eq:six-centre-step}
4+\Psi(w)\le b+\Psi(u).
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Computer-assisted proof]
Normalize a hypothetical occurrence by translation and coordinate permutation. Its selected vertices satisfy the degree conditions, the two-endpoint restriction, and the exact missing-corner counts. Lemma~\ref{lem:subset-low-degree} justifies the cycle exclusions. The counter identities in Appendix~\ref{app:counters} interpret the auxiliary threshold variables. Each catalogue key is discharged by a checked refutation or an implication from a previously refuted key. A refutation derives the empty clause from input clauses already proved necessary for the hypothetical snake. The archive assembles the reused refutations and 635 new certificates into the complete catalogue assertion.

There are 132 normalized induced eight-direction words. Each is checked against all $6^6$ count choices, covering $132\cdot6^6=6158592$ candidate transitions. The finite potential assertion is verified on every permitted transition. The geometric interpretation, catalogue coverage, potential check, and universal path summation are included in the completed contributor theorem and its saved dependency audit~\cite{bound235archive}. The independent numerical reconstruction retains 3546419 permitted transitions and verifies a potential of range five; the weaker bound seven above is the bound used in the checked universal argument. An independent rebuild of the entire contributor dependency closure remains pending.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Singleton estimate with boundary terms]\label{prop:count-235}
Every nontrivial $m$-edge snake in $\Q_9$ satisfies
\begin{equation}\label{eq:count-235}
4m\le T+21.
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Suppose $m\ge7$. Write $d_i$ for the direction joining $x_i$ to $x_{i+1}$. At index $i$, the state records $d_i,\ldots,d_{i+6}$ and $b_{i+2},\ldots,b_{i+6}$. Its next transition appends $b_{i+7}$. Summing~\eqref{eq:six-centre-step} over the $m-7$ transitions gives
\[
4(m-7)+\Psi_{m-7}\le\sum_{j=7}^{m-1}b_j+\Psi_0.
\]
The first six internal singleton counts contribute at least six and the two endpoints contribute at least eight. Hence $\sum_{j=7}^{m-1}b_j\le T-14$. Using $0\le\Psi\le7$ gives
\[
4m-28\le T-14+7=T-7,
\]
which proves the assertion. For $1\le m<7$, positivity and the endpoint bounds give $T\ge m+7$, and $4m\le m+28\le T+21$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Retained empty-square bound]\label{thm:upper235}
Every nontrivial $m$-edge snake in $\Q_9$ satisfies
\begin{equation}\label{eq:retained-empty-235}
\boxed{39m+2s_0\le9200.}
\end{equation}
In particular, $m\le235$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Substitute $T\ge4m-21$ into $T+2s_0+35(m+1)=9214$. This gives $39m+2s_0+14\le9214$. Since $s_0\ge0$ and $39\cdot236=9204>9200$, integrality gives $m\le235$. The zero-edge path satisfies the upper bound directly. Together with Theorem~\ref{thm:lower}, this proves Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Eighteen-empty-square target]\label{cor:eighteen-empty}
A snake with at least eighteen empty squares has at most 234 edges. It therefore suffices to prove $s_0\ge18$ for every hypothetical 235-edge snake in order to improve the universal upper bound to 234.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Equation~\eqref{eq:retained-empty-235} gives $39m\le9164<39\cdot235=9165$. The threshold cannot be reduced using this inequality alone: the numerical pair $(m,s_0)=(235,17)$ satisfies it. This numerical consistency asserts no realizing snake. The retained-identity arithmetic and the threshold implications have been checked in Lean under their stated count and incidence premises.
\end{proof}
The following sections retain the empty-square term through local share inequalities. They also describe restrictions on singleton counts needed to interpret those inequalities. The earlier 236 proof is preserved in Appendix~\ref{app:bound236}.

\section{Sharp restrictions on two singleton counts}\label{sec:distance-three-pair}
The next inequalities follow from explicit six-cycles near two selected vertices. The resulting inequalities impose no restrictions on the four intervening singleton counts.

\begin{proposition}[Distance-three six-cycle inequalities]\label{prop:distance-three-pair}
Let $x_0,x_1,\ldots,x_m$ be a snake in $\Q_9$, and suppose $r+7\le m$.
If its seven transitions starting at $x_r$ are
\[
(a,b,c,d,e,b,a),
\]
where $a,b,c,d,e$ are distinct coordinates, then
\[
\alpha_S(x_{r+1})+\alpha_S(x_{r+6})\ge10.
\]
If the transitions are $(a,b,c,d,e,b,f)$ with six distinct coordinates, then
\[
\alpha_S(x_{r+1})+\alpha_S(x_{r+6})\ge9.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Put $x=x_{r+1}$ and $y=x_{r+6}$. Write $e_i$ for the unit vector in coordinate $i$, and use addition in $\mathbb F_2^9$. The five transitions from $x$ to $y$ give
\[
y=x+e_c+e_d+e_e.
\]
The path directions at $x$ are $a,b$. Those at $y$ are $b,a$ in the first case and $b,f$ in the second. Thus $c,d,e$ are free directions at both vertices.

For each pair of coordinates from $\{c,d,e\}$, its opposite corner at $x$ is a neighbor of $y$ in the remaining coordinate. That neighbor is unselected, since it is outside the two path directions at $y$. The other two corners of the square at $x$ are also unselected. This gives three singleton squares at $x$ and, symmetrically, three at $y$.

For any coordinate $h$ outside the union of the displayed coordinates, set
\[
A_i=x+e_i+e_h,\qquad B_i=y+e_i+e_h\qquad(i\in\{c,d,e\}).
\]
These six vertices form the induced cycle
\[
A_c,\ B_d,\ A_e,\ B_c,\ A_d,\ B_e,\ A_c.
\]
They cannot all be selected in an induced path. Each missing vertex is the opposite corner of a singleton square at $x$ or $y$, because $i,h$ are free directions there. The cycles for different $h$ are vertex-disjoint, and their opposite corners are distinct from the six already counted. There are four choices of $h$ in the first case and three in the second. The respective totals are therefore at least $6+4=10$ and $6+3=9$.

Both inequalities have passed Lean, including their application to arbitrary distinct coordinate labels. The checked counting constraints retain every opposite-corner occurrence, and their geometric interpretation uses the singleton counts of the original snake.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Sharpness of the distance-three constants]\label{prop:pair-sharpness}
The constants $10$ and $9$ in Proposition~\ref{prop:distance-three-pair} are best possible over all snakes in $\Q_9$ satisfying the respective direction-pattern hypotheses.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The explicit transition sequences in Appendix~\ref{app:pair-sharpness} give the following witnesses, with vertices indexed from zero:
\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c|c}
\text{pattern}&\text{edge length}&r&\alpha_S(x_{r+1})&\alpha_S(x_{r+6})\\\hline
(a,b,c,d,e,b,a)&143&67&6&4\\
(a,b,c,d,e,b,f)&138&70&5&4.
\end{array}
\]
For each sequence, all vertices are distinct and two vertices are adjacent exactly when consecutive. The displayed seven-transition pattern occurs at the stated index. These properties and both singleton counts have passed Lean. The sums attain the respective lower bounds, so increasing either universal constant gives a counterexample. This last implication has also been checked.
\end{proof}


\section{A sharp pair inequality and its endpoint obstruction}\label{sec:endpoint-pair}
The neighboring transitions around two counted vertices can change the best possible inequality. The next result uses the full eight-transition segment. Its equality case also identifies a specific endpoint demand, which explains why distance from the endpoints permits a stronger bound.

\begin{theorem}[Outer-transition pair bound]\label{thm:outer-pair}
Let $P=(x_0,\ldots,x_m)$ be a snake and let $r+8\le m$. If its eight transitions starting at $x_r$ are
\[
(a,b,c,d,e,a,d,b),
\]
where the five named coordinates are distinct, then
\[
\alpha_{r+2}+\alpha_{r+6}\ge7.
\]
The constant seven is best possible under these hypotheses.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Computer-assisted proof and explicit sharpness]
Translate $x_r$ to zero and send $(a,b,c,d,e)$ to $(0,1,2,3,4)$. The segment becomes
\[
(0,1,3,7,15,31,30,22,20).
\]
Its counted vertices are 3 and 30. Their difference has four nonzero binary digits. At each centre, the two prescribed path neighbors exclude two coordinates from its singleton-square count. List the 21 opposite corners from the other seven coordinates, and concatenate the two lists. By Lemma~\ref{lem:window-missing-corners}, the number of absent entries, with multiplicity, equals $\alpha_{r+2}+\alpha_{r+6}$.

Suppose that sum is at most six. In the radius-four neighborhood of the internal centres, impose this joint upper bound together with the necessary degree, endpoint, square-cycle, and six-cycle restrictions of Section~\ref{sec:upper-proof}. A snake realizing the segment supplies the selected-vertex assignment and its actual endpoint indicators; the threshold variables are assigned their actual partial counts. The retained input has 1076 clauses on 879 variables. A refutation with 594 derived clauses and 34159 antecedent references derives the empty clause. Its replay, the interpretation of each retained clause, and the invariance of both singleton counts under the normalization have passed Lean~\cite{outerpairarchive}. The assumed sum is therefore impossible. No hypotheses on the other five counts enter the proof.

The 140-edge snake in Appendix~\ref{app:endpoint-witnesses} has this segment at $r=2$, with $(\alpha_4,\alpha_8)=(4,3)$. Distinctness, induced adjacency, the segment, and both counts have passed Lean. Its sum is seven, proving sharpness.
\end{proof}

The two outer transitions cannot both be omitted while retaining this bound. Deleting them leaves, after coordinate renaming, the six-transition pattern $(0,1,2,3,4,2)$, with the counted positions now at offsets one and five. The 146-edge snake in Appendix~\ref{app:endpoint-witnesses} realizes that shorter pattern with counts $(3,3)$. Its Lean-checked sum is six. Thus the full segment in Theorem~\ref{thm:outer-pair} is an essential part of the stated result.

\subsection{The endpoint demand set}
For the same eight-transition segment, put $x=x_{r+2}$ and
\[
\begin{aligned}
F&=\{0,\ldots,8\}\setminus\{b,c\},\\
G_r&=\left\{x+e_i+e_j:
\{i,j\}\in\binom F2\setminus\bigl\{\{a,e\},\{d,e\}\bigr\}\right\}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Here addition is in $\mathbb F_2^9$. The set $F$ consists of the seven directions unused by the path at $x$. Its 21 coordinate pairs give distinct opposite corners, and the two removed pairs are distinct, so $|G_r|=19$. Every member of $G_r$ is at Hamming distance two from $x$.

In the normalized segment, the removed corners are 18 and 27. The vertex 18 is adjacent to the internal vertex 22, whose segment neighbors are 30 and 20. The vertex 27 is adjacent to the internal vertex 31, whose segment neighbors are 15 and 30. Each removed corner would therefore give an internal vertex a third selected neighbor. Both are absent. The demand set consists of the remaining eligible opposite corners.

\begin{theorem}[Endpoint demand at equality]\label{thm:endpoint-demand}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:outer-pair}, if
\[
\alpha_{r+2}+\alpha_{r+6}\le7,
\]
then $G_r$ contains an endpoint of $P$. In particular,
\[
\Ham(x_{r+2},x_0)=2\quad\text{or}\quad
\Ham(x_{r+2},x_m)=2.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Computer-assisted proof]
Normalize the segment as in Theorem~\ref{thm:outer-pair}. Its first counted vertex is 3. The set $G_r$ becomes
\[
\begin{gathered}
\{10,34,43,51,66,75,83,99,130,139,147,163,195,\\
258,267,275,291,323,387\}.
\end{gathered}
\]
Assume that none of these 19 vertices is an endpoint. Assign an endpoint indicator to every represented vertex, true precisely at an endpoint of the full snake. The assumed absence supplies 19 negative unit clauses. Combine these with the ordinary local path conditions and the joint count cap seven. The extracted input consists of 1046 ordinary clauses and these 19 units, on 826 active variables. Its refutation has 733 derived clauses and 23414 antecedent references.

The ordinary clauses follow from the selected-vertex degrees, the bound of two endpoints, the forbidden square and six-cycles, and the actual missing-corner counts. Each added unit clause is true by the assumed endpoint absence. A generic interpretation of these units and the complete refutation have passed Lean~\cite{endpointarchive}. Hence the hypothetical assignment cannot exist: an endpoint belongs to the displayed set. Translation and the inverse coordinate permutation carry that endpoint back into $G_r$. These maps preserve which two coordinates differ, so its Hamming distance from $x_{r+2}$ is exactly two. The arbitrary-coordinate distance implication has also passed Lean.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Sharp bound away from the endpoints]\label{cor:endpoint-free-pair}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:outer-pair}, if both endpoints have Hamming distance at least three from $x_{r+2}$, then
\[
\alpha_{r+2}+\alpha_{r+6}\ge8.
\]
The constant eight is best possible with this additional hypothesis.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
A sum smaller than eight is at most seven, since the counts are integers. Theorem~\ref{thm:endpoint-demand} would give an endpoint at distance two, contradicting the hypothesis. For sharpness, the 148-edge snake in Appendix~\ref{app:endpoint-witnesses} has the prescribed segment at $r=74$, counts $(\alpha_{76},\alpha_{80})=(5,3)$, and Hamming distance four from $x_{76}$ to each endpoint. The full path, both counts, the segment, and both distances have passed Lean.
\end{proof}

The distance hypothesis concerns the cube metric. Separation by many edges along the path does not imply it. Nor can the hypothesis be weakened to endpoint distance at least two: the 140-edge sharpness example has endpoint distances two and four and still has pair sum seven. This distinction is checked on the complete witness.

\subsection{Overlapping endpoint demands}
The sets $G_r$ from Theorem~\ref{thm:endpoint-demand} must draw their required endpoints from the same two-element set. The following accounting principle makes this shared restriction explicit. It also applies to other local endpoint demands.
\begin{proposition}[Weighted endpoint accounting]\label{prop:endpoint-accounting}
Let $P$ be a nontrivial snake, and let $(G_i)_{i\in I}$ be a finite family of vertex sets, each containing at least one endpoint of $P$. Assign nonnegative rational weights $w_i$. If
\[
\sum_{i:\,x\in G_i} w_i\le1
\quad\text{for every vertex }x,
\]
then $\sum_{i\in I}w_i\le2$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Let $B$ be the two-element endpoint set. Each $G_i$ meets $B$, so
\[
\sum_i w_i\le\sum_i\sum_{x\in B\cap G_i}w_i
=\sum_{x\in B}\sum_{i:\,x\in G_i}w_i\le2.
\]
The integer-weight version, obtained by clearing a common denominator, has been checked in Lean.
\end{proof}

For example, suppose five distinct candidate vertices $u_0,\ldots,u_4$ yield demands $G_i=\{u_i,u_{i+1}\}$, with subscripts taken modulo five. Giving each demand weight $1/2$ satisfies the per-vertex condition, but the total weight is $5/2$, a contradiction. No three of these pairs are disjoint, so a test limited to three disjoint endpoint demands would miss this obstruction. The five-pair contradiction is also checked independently in Lean. Identifying a forced occurrence of this arrangement, or another weighted obstruction, in low-singleton geometry is an open research direction. The counting principle is standard; no claim of historical novelty is made for it.

Applying this proposition requires weights for which the bound holds at every cube vertex. Demands from different windows may intersect, so counting every demand as a different endpoint is invalid. The theorem supplies the demand sets; a further overlap estimate is needed for a stronger aggregate bound.

\section{Retaining empty squares through integer shares}\label{sec:empty-packing}
Let $S$ be the vertex set of a nontrivial snake, let $v=|S|$, and let $s_0$ be the number of empty coordinate squares. For $q\in\Q_9$, define
\[
e(q)=\#\{\text{empty coordinate squares containing }q\},
\qquad d(q)=|N(q)\cap S|.
\]
Then $\sum_q e(q)=4s_0$. For a positive integer $M$, assign to each selected vertex the integer share
\[
\sigma_M(x)=\sum_{q\sim x}\left\lfloor\frac{M e(q)}{d(q)}\right\rfloor.
\]
For $x\in S$ every denominator in this sum is positive. When summing over all cube vertices, define a zero-denominator contribution to be zero.

\begin{proposition}[Corner-share loss identity]\label{prop:share-loss}
Set $r_M(q)$ equal to the remainder of $M e(q)$ on division by $d(q)$ when $d(q)>0$, and set $r_M(q)=M e(q)$ when $d(q)=0$. Then
\[
\sum_{x\in S}\sigma_M(x)+\sum_{q\in\Q_9}r_M(q)=4M s_0.
\]
Consequently, with $T=\sum_{x\in S}\alpha_S(x)$,
\[
G_M:=2MT+\sum_{x\in S}\sigma_M(x),
\qquad G_M+70Mv\le18428M.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Interchanging the neighbor sums gives
\[
\sum_{x\in S}\sigma_M(x)=\sum_q d(q)\left\lfloor\frac{M e(q)}{d(q)}\right\rfloor.
\]
The division algorithm, together with the stated zero-degree convention, gives the first identity. Theorem~\ref{thm:exact-square} gives $T+2s_0+35v=9214$. Multiply this equality by $2M$ and use the nonnegativity of the remainder sum.
\end{proof}
The loss identity includes both rounding and empty corners without selected neighbors. The identity and its geometric interpretation have been checked in Lean. In particular, no positive contribution is assigned to an empty corner with no selected neighbor.

\begin{lemma}[Weighted window packing]\label{lem:share-packing}
Let $\sigma_j\ge0$ be shares at finitely many positions. Suppose that a family of windows $I_t$ has bounds $b_t\le\sum_{j\in I_t}\sigma_j$. If rational weights $w_t\ge0$ satisfy
\[
\sum_{t:\,j\in I_t}w_t\le1\quad\text{for every position }j,
\]
then $\sum_t w_t b_t\le\sum_j\sigma_j$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Multiply each window inequality by $w_t$, sum, and interchange the two finite sums:
\[
\sum_t w_t b_t\le\sum_j\sigma_j\sum_{t:\,j\in I_t}w_t\le\sum_j\sigma_j.
\]
Clearing a common denominator gives the integer statement checked in Lean.
\end{proof}
For five-position windows on a line, a useful integer choice selects disjoint windows. If $b_i$ bounds the share on positions $i,\ldots,i+4$, set $D(0)=0$ and
\[
D(n)=
\begin{cases}
D(n-1),&1\le n<5,\\
\max\{D(n-1),D(n-5)+b_{n-5}\},&n\ge5.
\end{cases}
\]
Induction gives $D(n)\le\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\sigma_j$: the first alternative uses nonnegativity, and the second adds the last five positions to a bound on the preceding positions. This implication has also been checked in Lean. Zero may be used for any window whose lower bound is unknown.

\subsection{A forced three-square contribution}
\begin{proposition}[Three-square share bonus]\label{prop:three-square-bonus}
Suppose a snake has seven consecutive transitions $(a,b,c,d,e,b,f)$ starting at $x_r$, where the six named coordinates are distinct. Put $x=x_{r+1}$ and $y=x_{r+6}$. Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\sigma_{12}(x)+\sigma_{12}(y)&\ge12,\\
24\bigl(\alpha_S(x)+\alpha_S(y)\bigr)+\sigma_{12}(x)+\sigma_{12}(y)&\ge228.
\end{aligned}
\]
No equality assumption on the singleton counts is required.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Use addition in $\mathbb F_2^9$ and the unit vectors $e_i$. The six internal vertices of the segment are
\[
\begin{gathered}
x,\quad x+e_b,\quad x+e_b+e_c,\quad x+e_b+e_c+e_d,\\
x+e_b+e_c+e_d+e_e,\quad y=x+e_c+e_d+e_e.
\end{gathered}
\]
Each already has two neighbors in the segment. Every other neighbor of these internal vertices is therefore unselected.

For a base vertex $q$ and coordinates $i,j$, write
$Q(q;i,j)=\{q,q+e_i,q+e_j,q+e_i+e_j\}$.
The preceding observation forces all vertices of the following three squares to be unselected:
\[
Q(x+e_d;b,e),\qquad Q(x+e_e;b,c),\qquad Q(x+e_e;b,d).
\]
The four vertices in the first column below are neighbors of $x$ or $y$. The listed directions lead to three unselected neighbors of each such vertex. Thus its selected-neighbor count is at most six. The three empty squares give the displayed lower counts for $e(q)$.
\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c|c}
q&\text{absent directions}&e(q)\ge&d(q)\le&
\left\lfloor12e(q)/d(q)\right\rfloor\ge\\\hline
x+e_d&b,c,e&1&6&2\\
x+e_e&b,c,d&2&6&4\\
y+e_c&b,d,e&2&6&4\\
y+e_d&b,c,e&1&6&2
\end{array}
\]
Each denominator is positive because the corresponding vertex is adjacent to $x$ or $y$. The four contributions are distinct summands in the two shares. Their sum is at least $2+4+4+2=12$. Proposition~\ref{prop:distance-three-pair} gives a singleton-count sum of at least nine, hence the charge is at least $24\cdot9+12=228$.

The share inequality, its application to arbitrary snakes and coordinate labels, and the combined charge inequality have passed Lean. The independently checked refutation uses 115 Boolean clauses and only the selected segment and the selected-vertex degree restriction; it uses no singleton-count equality or cycle exclusion. Its resolution certificate has 281 antecedent references.
\end{proof}

The bonus retains an empty-square contribution even where the count-only inequality is sharp. Since the share proof uses only local degree restrictions, it also applies to selected sets containing cycles. It does not by itself overcome the cycle obstruction to purely local bounds. The constant 12 is not asserted to be sharp.

\begin{corollary}[Occurrence share bound]\label{cor:occurrence-share}
Let $J$ be any set of starting indices for occurrences satisfying Proposition~\ref{prop:three-square-bonus}. Then
\[
\sum_{x\in S}\sigma_{12}(x)\ge6|J|,
\qquad G_{12}\ge24T+6|J|.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Sum the local lower bound 12 over $r\in J$. A position $k$ can appear in the charged pair only for starts $r=k-1$ or $r=k-6$, so each share is counted at most twice. Thus $12|J|\le2\sum_{x\in S}\sigma_{12}(x)$. The definition of $G_{12}$ gives the second inequality. The multiplicity bound, its application to arbitrary snakes, and the charge deduction have passed Lean.
\end{proof}

A lower bound on the frequency of qualifying occurrences is still needed for a new uniform upper bound. Combining this result with other share bounds must respect a common per-vertex capacity; their contributions cannot simply be added when their charged positions overlap.

\subsection{Five-centre share inequalities}\label{sec:five-shares}
Write $\Sigma=\sum_{x\in S}\sigma_{12}(x)$. Proposition~\ref{prop:share-loss} gives $\Sigma\le48s_0$ and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:share-budget-12}
G_{12}=24T+\Sigma,\qquad G_{12}+840(m+1)\le221136.
\end{equation}

\begin{theorem}[Five-centre share bounds]\label{thm:five-shares}
Suppose six consecutive directions beginning at $x_r$ and the five internal singleton counts satisfy a row of the following table. Distinct letters denote distinct coordinates; each of $A,B,C$ that occurs lies in $[6,21]$. Then $\sum_{j=1}^{5}\sigma_{12}(x_{r+j})\ge L$, with $L$ as displayed.
\[
\begin{array}{c|c|r}
\text{directions}&(\alpha_{r+1},\ldots,\alpha_{r+5})&L\\\hline
(a,b,c,d,b,e)&(3,3,3,A,5)&48\\
(a,b,c,d,e,f)&(3,A,2,3,B)&96\\
(a,b,c,a,d,e)&(A,3,B,3,3)&96\\
(a,b,c,d,e,f)&(4,A,2,3,B)&96\\
(a,b,c,d,a,e)&(A,B,2,2,C)&96
\end{array}
\]
Every row also holds with the direction word and count tuple both reversed.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Computer-assisted proof]
Use the union of radius-four neighborhoods around the five centres. All neighbors of each centre, all squares incident to those neighbors, and all vertices needed for their selected-neighbor counts are represented. A hypothetical snake therefore determines the exact integer shares as well as the singleton counts in this model. Since each centre is selected, every denominator in its share is positive. Threshold counters encode the empty-square and selected-neighbor counts; their interpretation gives exactly $\lfloor12e(q)/d(q)\rfloor$ for each summand.

Assume the sum is at most $L-1$. The path, count, share, and cap clauses then have a refutation. In table order, the trimmed cores contain 6681, 7208, 14471, 11582, and 11751 clauses. Each retained clause is proved necessary for the hypothetical snake, and each refutation is replayed by the sound checker. The geometric interpretations and arbitrary-coordinate applications have completed Lean checks~\cite{recentarchive}. This proves the five bounds. Reversal preserves the selected set and hence $e(q)$, $d(q)$, and every vertex share; it reverses precisely the five counted centres. The sum is unchanged. The reversal implication is checked separately.
\end{proof}
Ordinary local forests matching these five rows have share sums 263, 307, 378, 855, and 450, respectively. They show that the corresponding ordinary local conditions are consistent. They establish neither full-snake realizability nor sharpness of the stated share bounds.

\begin{corollary}[Sufficient occurrence counts]\label{cor:share-occurrences-234}
Nine pairwise disjoint five-centre occurrences with $L=96$ imply $m\le234$. Alternatively, 43 such occurrences with distinct starting positions suffice, allowing overlap.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Disjoint occurrences give $\Sigma\ge9\cdot96=864$, hence $s_0\ge18$. For arbitrary distinct starts, any position lies in at most five windows, so $5\Sigma\ge43\cdot96=4128$. Combining with $\Sigma\le48s_0$ gives $240s_0\ge4128>240\cdot17$, again $s_0\ge18$. Apply Corollary~\ref{cor:eighteen-empty}. The numerical implications and overlap accounting have passed Lean. Neither required occurrence frequency is established for arbitrary snakes.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Short return and low-count restrictions}\label{sec:short-returns}
In the following statements a direction word starts at $x_r$, and distinct letters denote distinct coordinates. The singleton counts refer to the full snake.

\begin{proposition}[Three exclusions of a singleton count]\label{prop:three-singleton-exclusions}
The following implications hold without restrictions on other counts:
\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
\text{directions}&\text{conclusion}\\\hline
(a,b,c,d,a,e)&\alpha_{r+4}\ge2\\
(a,b,c,d,a,e,b)&\alpha_{r+1}\ge2\\
(a,b,c,d,a,c,e,d)&\alpha_{r+7}\ge2
\end{array}
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Computer-assisted proof]
Impose the indicated count at most one in the necessary radius-four model. The three refutations have respectively 282, 456, and 829 input clauses. The missing-corner interpretation, all retained path clauses, refutations, and transport to arbitrary labels pass the recorded Lean checks~\cite{recentarchive}. Positivity then gives each asserted bound. For the first row there is also a geometric explanation: at $x=x_{r+4}$ the opposite corner $x+e_b+e_c=x_{r+1}+e_d$ is unselected. If this were the unique missing pair, the singleton continuation restriction would force the second step along the arm through $x_{r+5}$ to use $b$ or $c$. Its prescribed coordinate is $e$, a contradiction. The finite proof independently checks the needed exclusion. Sharpness of two for complete snakes is unresolved.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Compatibility of small counts]\label{prop:small-count-compatibility}
The following implications hold:
\[
\begin{array}{c|l}
(a,b,c,d,a,b)&\alpha_{r+1}+\alpha_{r+5}\ge5\\
(a,b,c,d,a,e,b,a)&\alpha_{r+4}+\alpha_{r+7}\ge6\\
(a,b,c,d,e,c)&\alpha_{r+1}\le3\ \Longrightarrow\ \alpha_{r+5}\ge3.
\end{array}
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Computer-assisted proof]
For the first two rows, a joint threshold counter represents the indicated sum, with multiplicity preserved if a Boolean variable contributes at both centres. Adding the contrary bounds four and five gives refuted cores of 2228 and 1467 clauses. The third row excludes the simultaneous caps three and two with a 1094-clause core. The additional cycle exclusions in the first two cases follow from Lemma~\ref{lem:subset-low-degree}. All clause interpretations, replays, and arbitrary-coordinate implications have passed Lean~\cite{recentarchive}. Local forests attain the first two lower bounds; complete-snake sharpness is unproved. The third assertion is directional: reversing it requires reversing the full direction word as well as the counts.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp return inequalities]\label{thm:sharp-returns}
For consecutive directions $(a,b,c,a)$,
\[
\alpha_{r+1}+\alpha_{r+3}\ge5.
\]
For consecutive directions $(a,b,c,d,e,a)$,
\[
\alpha_{r+1}+\alpha_{r+4}+\alpha_{r+5}\ge13.
\]
Both constants are attained by complete snakes in $\Q_9$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Computer-assisted proof]
The contrary sum caps four and twelve are excluded in the necessary geometric models by replayed refutations and checked counter interpretations. The second refutation has 3593 core clauses and 10807572 antecedent references. The arbitrary-coordinate deductions and complete witnesses are checked in Lean~\cite{recentarchive}. The 146-edge witness in Appendix~\ref{app:return-witnesses} attains counts $(2,3)$ at offsets one and three of a segment starting at index 95. The 143-edge witness attains $(3,5,5)$ at offsets one, four, and five of a segment starting at index 64. Thus neither constant admits a universal increase under its stated direction hypothesis.
\end{proof}

A complete six-direction survey considers the nine induced words up to coordinate relabelling and reversal, ten pairs of internal positions, and four caps $(2,2),(2,3),(3,2),(3,3)$. Of the 360 tests, 50 have checked exclusions and 310 have verified acyclic local assignments. The exclusions follow from 33 replayed shorter premises. Appendix~\ref{app:pair-caps} records the maximal forbidden caps. A positive local assignment proves feasibility of the local conditions only. It need not extend to a complete snake. Neither this catalogue nor the sharp triple inequality removes the earlier model's mean-$42/11$ obstruction by itself.

\section{Deriving empty-square bounds from local geometry}\label{sec:forced-charge}
The share definition uses the full selected set $S$, but a lower bound can often be proved from a short selected segment. The essential fact is that enlarging the selected set can only decrease the relevant share. This permits finite calculations on a superset that need not itself be a snake.

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity under enlarging the selected set]\label{lem:share-monotonicity}
For $A\subseteq\Q_9$, let $e_A(q)$ count coordinate squares through $q$ disjoint from $A$, and let $d_A(q)=|N(q)\cap A|$. If $S\subseteq U$ and $x\in S$, then
\[
\sum_{q\sim x}\left\lfloor\frac{12e_U(q)}{d_U(q)}\right\rfloor
\le\sigma_{12}(x).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Every denominator is positive because $x$ belongs to both sets. For each $q\sim x$, inclusion gives $e_U(q)\le e_S(q)$ and $d_S(q)\le d_U(q)$. Hence $e_U(q)/d_U(q)\le e_S(q)/d_S(q)$. Multiplication by 12, taking the floor, and summation preserve the inequality. The positive-denominator argument and the corresponding integer inequalities have been checked in Lean~\cite{geometricarchive}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Degree-forced absence]\label{lem:degree-forced}
Let $A\subseteq S$ be known selected vertices. If the induced subgraph on $A\cup\{q\}$ has a vertex of degree at least three, then $q\notin S$. In particular, every neighbour outside $A$ of a vertex having two neighbours in $A$ is absent.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $q\in S$, then $A\cup\{q\}\subseteq S$. A vertex of degree at least three in this subset would have degree at least three in the graph induced by $S$, contradicting the snake condition. This also covers the case where $q$ itself has three neighbours in $A$.
\end{proof}

Let $W=(x_r,\ldots,x_{r+n})$ be an induced segment and let $D$ be any set of vertices proved absent using this lemma. Put $U=\Q_9\setminus D$. For a set $C\subseteq W$ of five internal centres define
\begin{equation}\label{eq:absence-share}
L_D(C)=\sum_{x\in C}\sum_{q\sim x}
\left\lfloor\frac{12\,\#\{Q:q\in Q,\ Q\subseteq D\}}
{9-|N(q)\cap D|}\right\rfloor.
\end{equation}
The numerator counts coordinate squares. The denominator is positive because $x\in C\subseteq S\subseteq U$.

\begin{theorem}[Forced-absence share bound]\label{thm:forced-share}
With the preceding notation,
\[
L_D(C)\le\sum_{x\in C}\sigma_{12}(x).
\]
Increasing a justified absence set $D$ can only strengthen this lower bound. Several lower bounds on the same five-centre share may be combined by taking their maximum.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The complement $U$ contains $S$, and the expression in~\eqref{eq:absence-share} is precisely the left side of Lemma~\ref{lem:share-monotonicity}, summed over $C$. Increasing $D$ decreases $U$ and applies the same monotonicity argument. Every available bound is at most the same share sum, so their maximum is also at most that sum. Their sum would need a separate overlap argument.
\end{proof}

The construction is invariant under translations and coordinate permutations. For eight-transition segments, choose internal positions 3 through 7. Direct enumeration gives 132 direction shapes up to relabelling, 76 with positive bounds, and maximum bound 18. For nine-transition segments, choose positions 4 through 8. There are 505 shapes, 357 with positive bounds; 231 improve on the corresponding eight-transition suffix, and the maximum is 54. All positive instances and their arbitrary-snake interpretations have passed Lean. The independent calculation enumerates all 4608 coordinate squares, every denominator and every summand. The formula also defines the zero entries, so no unlisted shape is assigned a positive bound~\cite{geometricarchive}.

For longer segments, the same argument applies to any five selected centres. It supplies 20 checked ten-transition instances on an earlier attaining cycle, and 96 positive ten- or eleven-transition instances on the complete critical family discussed in Section~\ref{sec:restricted-family}. These are local inequalities for arbitrary matching segments; membership in a particular finite family is unnecessary for their validity.

\subsection{Finite alternatives and complete neighbour choices}
An absent-vertex calculation can be strengthened by branching on a vertex whose membership in $S$ is unknown. In the absent alternative, add that vertex to $D$. In the selected alternative, add it to $A$ and apply Lemma~\ref{lem:degree-forced}. A bound valid in both alternatives holds for the original segment.

\begin{proposition}[Exhaustive local choice bound]\label{prop:local-choice-share}
Suppose a finite list of alternatives covers every selected set satisfying the stated local hypotheses. In each alternative, derive either a contradiction or an absence set giving a share bound $L_t$ on the same centres $C$. If every surviving alternative has $L_t\ge L$, then $\sum_{x\in C}\sigma_{12}(x)\ge L$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The actual selected set satisfies one of the alternatives. It cannot satisfy an alternative proved contradictory. In its surviving alternative, Theorem~\ref{thm:forced-share} gives the claimed inequality.
\end{proof}

A useful complete list is obtained from each end of a local segment. That end already has one selected neighbour in the segment and can have at most one further selected neighbour. After removing vertices already known absent, enumerate the alternatives of no further selected neighbour and each individual candidate neighbour. Enumerate these choices jointly at the two local ends. Every actual snake satisfies one joint choice. These ends are local segment ends and may be internal vertices of the full snake; no global endpoint-demand assumption is used.

Single-vertex splits give eight stronger bounds on the nine-transition critical windows and 38 on the eleven-transition windows. Complete neighbour choices give 34 further improvements on eleven-transition windows. The last calculation checks 11,554 joint configurations on 214 distinct direction shapes. The arbitrary-snake inequalities are obtained by the preceding exhaustive alternatives and degree-forcing argument. Their individual finite checks and choice coverage have passed Lean~\cite{choicearchive}.

\subsection{Saturation of a singleton-count cap}
At an internal vertex $x$, let $K$ be its 21 opposite corners from coordinate pairs avoiding the incoming and outgoing directions. Lemma~\ref{lem:window-missing-corners} gives $\alpha_S(x)=|K\setminus S|$.

\begin{proposition}[Count saturation]\label{prop:count-saturation}
Suppose $D\cap S=\varnothing$, $A\subseteq S$, and $\ell\le\alpha_S(x)\le h$. Then
\[
|K\cap(D\cup\{q\})|>h\ \Longrightarrow\ q\in S,
\]
and
\[
|K\cap(A\cup\{q\})|>21-\ell\ \Longrightarrow\ q\notin S.
\]
The inequalities $|K\cap D|>h$ or $|K\cap A|>21-\ell$ directly contradict the hypotheses.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
For the first implication, if $q\notin S$, every vertex in $K\cap(D\cup\{q\})$ is an absent corner, contradicting the upper bound $h$. For the second, if $q\in S$, all vertices in $K\cap(A\cup\{q\})$ are selected. Since $|K|=21$, the number of absent corners is then smaller than $\ell$. The terminal contradictions are the same counts without $q$. These statements, their iteration with degree-forced absence, and their application to arbitrary snakes have passed Lean~\cite{choicearchive}.
\end{proof}

The deduction process alternates selected and absent facts. A count cap may force new selected corners; these selected vertices can force further absences and empty squares. Each step preserves facts true of every selected set under consideration. This justifies a finite trace ending in a share bound or contradiction.

The following nine consequences require only the indicated upper bounds. Write $A_j=\alpha_{r+j}$, and represent distinct coordinate labels by distinct digits. Every row bounds $\sum_{j=6}^{10}\sigma_{12}(x_{r+j})$. All other singleton counts are unrestricted beyond their universal bounds.
\begin{theorem}[Count-conditioned five-centre shares]\label{thm:saturated-shares}
The direction word and count caps in each row imply the stated lower bound $L$:
\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{l l r}
\toprule
Eleven directions & Required upper bounds & $L$\\\midrule
$01203420150$ & $A_{6}\le2,\ A_{7}\le2$ & 19\\
$01203450310$ & $A_{5}\le2$ & 36\\
$01203450320$ & $A_{5}\le2$ & 15\\
$01231043125$ & $A_{7}\le2,\ A_{8}\le2$ & 13\\
$01231043152$ & $A_{7}\le2,\ A_{8}\le2$ & 26\\
$01234032513$ & $A_{8}\le2$ & 26\\
$01234032563$ & $A_{8}\le2$ & 12\\
$01234215036$ & $A_{5}\le1,\ A_{8}\le5,\ A_{9}\le1$ & 5\\
$01234215630$ & $A_{5}\le1,\ A_{6}\le5,\ A_{8}\le5,\ A_{9}\le1$ & 12\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Computer-assisted proof]
Normalize the segment by translation and coordinate permutation. Enumerate all joint neighbour choices at its two ends. Apply degree-forced absence and Proposition~\ref{prop:count-saturation} until either a contradiction is reached or an absence mask gives the required share. There are 443 checked branches across these nine rows. Only the count caps printed in the table are used; lower count bounds and unused upper bounds have been removed. The finite branch checks, semantic interpretation of every deduction, and arbitrary-coordinate transport have passed Lean. A separate reconstruction checks the 21-corner sets and all reduced absence masks~\cite{choicearchive}.
\end{proof}

\section{Direct count restrictions on longer segments}\label{sec:long-counts}
The same geometry gives short proofs of restrictions that were first encountered during share calculations. They do not require a large local refutation.

\begin{theorem}[Three forced opposite corners]\label{thm:three-forced-corners}
For six distinct coordinates $a,b,c,d,e,f$, each direction pattern beginning at $x_r$ has the stated consequence:
\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
\text{eleven directions}&\text{singleton count}\\\hline
(a,b,c,d,a,e,d,c,f,e,d)&\alpha_{r+10}\ge3\\
(a,b,c,d,e,f,d,c,a,b,d)&\alpha_{r+8}\ge3\\
(a,b,c,d,e,f,d,c,a,f,d)&\alpha_{r+10}\ge3
\end{array}
\]
No conditions on the other singleton counts are imposed.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Translate $x_r$ to zero and assign $a,b,c,d,e,f$ to coordinates $0,1,2,3,4,5$. The canonical selected vertices, target vertex, and three absent corners are listed below. Consecutive entries are joined by the prescribed transition.
\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{p{0.64\textwidth}r l}
\toprule
Selected vertices & Target & Opposite corners\\\midrule
$0,1,3,7,15,14,30,22,18,50,34,42$ & 34 & $6,33,39$\\
$0,1,3,7,15,31,63,55,51,50,48,56$ & 51 & $17,27,33$\\
$0,1,3,7,15,31,63,55,51,50,18,26$ & 18 & $6,17,23$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
In the first row, vertex 1 has selected neighbours 0 and 3, excluding 33. Vertex 7 has selected neighbours 3 and 15, excluding 6 and 39. In the second row, vertex 1 excludes 17 and 33, and vertex 31, with selected neighbours 15 and 63, excludes 27. In the third row, vertex 7 excludes 6 and 23, while vertex 1 excludes 17. Each exclusion is Lemma~\ref{lem:degree-forced}.

In table order, the free coordinate pairs producing these opposite corners at the target are
\[
\bigl((2,5),(0,1),(0,2)\bigr),\quad
\bigl((1,5),(3,5),(1,4)\bigr),\quad
\bigl((2,4),(0,1),(0,2)\bigr).
\]
These pairs avoid both directions incident to the target along the segment. Their corners are distinct and absent, so Lemma~\ref{lem:window-missing-corners} gives at least three singleton squares. Translation and permutation preserve all conditions. All three finite checks and the arbitrary-snake conclusions have passed Lean~\cite{choicearchive,newmodelarchive}.
\end{proof}

Ordinary induced-path validity also constrains longer direction windows. If a proposed segment selects every vertex of a finite cycle with two neighbours at each vertex, Lemma~\ref{lem:subset-low-degree} excludes it. This criterion proves 27 distinct twelve-vertex cycle exclusions in the recorded longer-window family. In particular, the words
\[
(0,1,2,3,1,0,4,1,3,2,1),\quad
(0,1,2,0,3,4,0,2,1,0,4),\quad
(0,1,2,3,4,2,1,0,2,4,3)
\]
would introduce an edge between the first and last vertices. The exclusions and their arbitrary-coordinate interpretations are checked; other direction words can replace them in a finite relaxation. These validity controls therefore support the share calculation without establishing that every low-charge cycle is impossible~\cite{geometricarchive}.

\section{Compatibility between overlapping windows}\label{sec:compatibility}
A local model for one window may have a solution that disagrees with every solution for its neighboring window on their shared vertices. The following exclusions impose the counts on a common selected-vertex set. They strengthen necessary conditions on snakes, although a global aggregate improvement still requires coverage of all permitted sequences.

\subsection{An ordinary five-centre exclusion}\label{sec:direct-share}
\begin{proposition}[Repeated-direction window exclusion]\label{prop:share-window-exclusion}
Let $a,b,c,d$ be four distinct coordinates. No snake in $\Q_9$ has six consecutive transition directions
\[
(a,b,c,a,d,b)
\]
whose five internal vertices have singleton-square counts $(2,4,5,3,3)$ in that order.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Translate the first vertex to zero and permute coordinates so that $(a,b,c,d)$ becomes $(0,1,2,3)$. The seven vertices then have binary integer labels
\[
(0,1,3,7,6,14,12).
\]
In the union of radius-four neighborhoods of the five internal vertices, impose the selected-vertex degree constraints inherited from an induced path, the limit of two endpoints, and the stated singleton-square counts. A finite encoding of these necessary conditions contains an inconsistent subset of 1,795 clauses on 1,134 active Boolean variables. Its resolution certificate has been replayed in Lean. The soundness of the clauses and the translation and coordinate-permutation argument have also been checked. The inconsistent subset contains no empty-square-share constraint, so the exclusion follows from the ordinary path conditions alone.
\end{proof}


\subsection{Seven centres with equal counts}\label{sec:share-overlap}
For the five-centre shapes
\[
A=(0,1,2,3,0,4),\qquad B=(0,1,2,3,0,1),
\]
three consecutive windows consisting of two copies of $A$ and one of $B$, up to normalization and reversal, reduce to the four patterns in the following proposition. This gives a concrete compatibility restriction stronger than separate treatment of each five-centre window.

\begin{proposition}[Seven-centre overlap exclusion]\label{prop:seven-overlap}
No snake in $\Q_9$ has seven consecutive internal vertices with singleton-square count four and one of the following eight-transition patterns, after coordinate renaming:
\[
\begin{aligned}
&(0,1,2,3,0,1,4,3), &&(0,1,2,3,0,4,2,3),\\
&(0,1,2,3,4,1,2,0), &&(0,1,2,3,4,1,2,5).
\end{aligned}
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Translate the initial vertex to zero. For each direction pattern, impose the seven singleton counts and the necessary induced-path degree and endpoint conditions in the union of the radius-four neighborhoods. The four resulting systems have inconsistent subsets with, respectively, 3,282, 2,982, 4,174, and 2,508 Boolean clauses. Their refutations have independently checked proof certificates. Each clause follows from the path conditions; no empty-square-share constraint or additional cycle cut is used.

For the relation with the three-window obstruction, normalize coordinate labels by first appearance. The first six directions then belong to the three patterns $(0,1,2,3,0,1)$, $(0,1,2,3,0,4)$, and $(0,1,2,3,4,1)$. Enumerating the two remaining normalized coordinate names, requiring an induced path, and requiring two windows of type $A$ and one of type $B$ leaves exactly the four patterns of Proposition~\ref{prop:seven-overlap}. The finite classification, the refutations, their geometric interpretations, and the combined statement have passed Lean, with coordinate normalization explicit in the combined statement.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Recovering the excess hidden by a coarse count}\label{sec:coarse-excess}
Write $D_0,D_1,D_2,D_3$ for the four direction patterns in Proposition~\ref{prop:seven-overlap}, in their displayed order. The symbol $H$ below denotes any integer in $[6,21]$; separate occurrences need not have the same value.

\begin{proposition}[Coarse-count correction]\label{prop:coarse-correction}
The seven internal singleton counts of a snake cannot have either of the following combinations:
\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
\text{directions}&\text{singleton counts}\\\hline
D_0&(3,3,H,3,3,H,3)\\
D_1&(3,H,3,3,H,3,3).
\end{array}
\]
For either $D_2$ or $D_3$, if the counts are
\[
(a,3,3,b,3,3,c),\qquad a,b,c\ge6,
\]
then $a+b+c\ge19$. In particular their seven-count total is at least $31$, and
\[
(a-6)+(b-6)+(c-6)\ge1.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
For $D_0$ and $D_1$, impose the displayed intervals in the same radius-four path model used above. Inconsistent subsets contain 1,273 and 1,223 clauses, respectively. For $D_2$ and $D_3$, impose the exact tuple $(6,3,3,6,3,3,6)$; inconsistent subsets contain 4,017 and 2,856 clauses. All four certificate replays and their interpretations for arbitrary snakes have passed Lean. These refutations use the ordinary degree, endpoint and singleton-count conditions, with no additional cycle cuts or share caps. If $a,b,c\ge6$ and their sum were at most $18$, each would equal six, contradicting the corresponding exact-tuple exclusion. The arithmetic consequences have also been checked.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Three-position excess accounting]\label{prop:three-excess}
Index all selected vertices by $j$, and put
\[
b_j=\min\{6,\alpha_S(x_j)\},\qquad
\delta_j=\alpha_S(x_j)-b_j.
\]
Let $J$ be any set of distinct starting indices of seven-centre windows satisfying the $D_2$ or $D_3$ hypotheses of Proposition~\ref{prop:coarse-correction}, with $r$ denoting the first internal centre. Then
\[
|J|\le3\sum_j\delta_j.
\]
Consequently the integer charge at $M=12$ satisfies
\[
G_{12}\ge24\sum_j b_j+8|J|+\sum_{x\in S}\sigma_{12}(x).
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Each qualifying window gives $\delta_r+\delta_{r+3}+\delta_{r+6}\ge1$. A fixed position $j$ occurs in these triples only for starts $j$, $j-3$ and $j-6$ that belong to $J$. Summing the window inequalities therefore gives
\[
|J|\le\sum_{r\in J}(\delta_r+\delta_{r+3}+\delta_{r+6})
\le3\sum_j\delta_j.
\]
Use $T=\sum_j b_j+\sum_j\delta_j$ and $G_{12}=24T+\sum_x\sigma_{12}(x)$. The general finite-set overlap inequality, its multiplicity-three specialization, the decomposition of $T$, and the resulting charge inequality have been checked in Lean.
\end{proof}
This count correction can be combined with the empty-square term without changing its definition. It does not yet give a positive density of qualifying windows in every snake. Other low-charge sequences must also be controlled before the charge targets in Proposition~\ref{prop:charge-targets} follow.

\begin{proposition}[Joint singleton excess]\label{prop:joint-singleton-excess}
For a seven-centre snake window with counts $(a,3,3,b,3,3,c)$, where $6\le a,b,c\le21$, the following stronger bounds hold:
\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{directions}&a+b+c& (a-6)+(b-6)+(c-6)\\\hline
D_2&\ge29&\ge11\\
D_3&\ge28&\ge10.
\end{array}
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
At each of the three high-count centres, exclude its two path directions. There are 21 pairs of remaining directions. A pair contributes a singleton square precisely when its opposite corner is unselected, since the other two corners are neighbors outside the induced path. Concatenating the three lists of opposite corners therefore counts $a+b+c$. Shared opposite-corner vertices must retain their multiplicities in these lists.

Impose a bound of 28 for $D_2$, or 27 for $D_3$, on this joint count. The necessary radius-four path conditions and cycle exclusions then contain inconsistent subsets of 4,376 and 4,080 Boolean clauses, respectively. The two proof certificates, the soundness of the joint counting constraint, and the equality between each normalized count and the corresponding count in an arbitrary snake have all been checked in Lean. Their refutations give the displayed lower bounds. Subtracting $18$ gives the excess inequalities.
\end{proof}

Let $J_2$ and $J_3$ be disjoint sets of qualifying starting positions for the two direction types. The same multiplicity-three accounting now gives
\[
11|J_2|+10|J_3|\le3\sum_j\delta_j,
\qquad
G_{12}\ge24\sum_j b_j+88|J_2|+80|J_3|+\sum_{x\in S}\sigma_{12}(x).
\]
The weighted finite-set inequality and the charge consequence have passed Lean. The saved diagnostic cycles in Appendix~\ref{app:diagnostics} avoid these qualifying windows. Increasing only these weights therefore leaves those particular cycles admissible.


\subsection{Seven centres with mixed counts}\label{sec:critical-overlap}
\begin{proposition}[Mixed-count overlap exclusions]\label{prop:mixed-overlap}
Let $a,b,c,d,e$ be five distinct coordinates. Neither row below can occur as eight consecutive transitions of a snake together with the indicated counts at its seven internal vertices:
\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
\text{transitions}&\text{singleton counts}\\\hline
(a,b,c,d,a,e,c,d)&(3,5,4,4,5,3,3)\\
(a,b,c,d,e,c,a,d)&(2,5,4,5,3,5,2).
\end{array}
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Translate the first vertex to zero and relabel the five coordinates by first occurrence. Restrict a hypothetical snake to the union of radius-four neighborhoods of the seven internal vertices. Its selected vertices satisfy the prescribed counts, degree at most two, the interior degree and endpoint conditions, and the exclusion of selected cycles. The singleton counts equal the numbers of absent eligible opposite corners.

For the first row, these necessary conditions give an inconsistent subset of 1,552 Boolean clauses on 1,155 active variables; for the second, the corresponding numbers are 421 and 407. The refutations use 67,720 and 1,023 antecedent references. The interpretation of the clauses, the certificate replays, and the application to arbitrary distinct coordinates have passed Lean. A hypothetical snake would therefore produce a satisfying assignment to an inconsistent formula, a contradiction.
\end{proof}


These exclusions remove two periodic sequences admitted by an earlier diagnostic model. A replacement sequence has the same recorded lower-table mean, so those removals alone give no smaller global bound. Appendix~\ref{app:diagnostics} records the finite computations and their scope.

\subsection{An asymmetric relation at Hamming distance four}\label{sec:distance-four}
The second mixed-count exclusion admits a stronger statement involving only two positions. This removes all hypotheses on the five intervening counts.

\begin{proposition}[Asymmetric singleton-count bounds]\label{prop:distance-four}
Suppose a snake contains eight consecutive transitions $(a,b,c,d,e,c,a,d)$ starting at $x_r$, with the five named coordinates distinct. Put
\[
x=x_{r+1},\qquad y=x_{r+7},\qquad X=\alpha_S(x),\qquad Y=\alpha_S(y).
\]
Then $\Ham(x,y)=4$ and
\[
X\ge2,\qquad X\le2\Longrightarrow Y\ge4,\qquad
Y\le1\Longrightarrow X\ge9.
\]
Consequently,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:distance-four-tradeoff}
X+Y\ge5,\qquad 2X+Y\ge8,\qquad X+6Y\ge15.
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The six transitions between $x$ and $y$ give $y=x+e_a+e_b+e_d+e_e$, proving the Hamming-distance statement. The path directions at $x$ are $a,b$, and those at $y$ are $a,d$.

Two singleton squares at $x$ can be identified directly. Their coordinate pairs are $\{c,d\}$ and $\{d,e\}$. Their opposite corners satisfy
\[
x+e_c+e_d=x_{r+4}+e_b,\qquad
x+e_d+e_e=x_{r+6}+e_b.
\]
Both are unselected: each is an additional neighbor of an internal segment vertex that already has its two path neighbors selected. The adjacent corners of these squares at $x$ are also unselected, since $c,d,e$ are outside its two path directions. Hence $X\ge2$. Similarly, the coordinate pair $\{c,e\}$ at $y$ has opposite corner $y+e_c+e_e=x_{r+4}+e_a$, which is unselected by the same degree restriction. Thus $Y\ge1$.

For the two conditional assertions, suppose respectively that $(X,Y)$ is bounded above by $(2,3)$ or $(8,1)$. Normalize the coordinate labels and restrict the snake to the union of radius-four neighborhoods of the seven internal centres. Impose these two upper bounds and leave every other internal count in its full interval $[0,21]$. The necessary degree, endpoint, and missing-corner conditions contain inconsistent subsets of 1464 and 1152 Boolean clauses. Their clause interpretations and contradiction certificates have been checked in Lean, including their application to arbitrary distinct coordinates. The smaller exclusion $(X,Y)\le(1,21)$ also has a checked 14-clause certificate, consistent with the direct two-square argument above. These checks are archived separately~\cite{pairarchive}.

It remains to deduce~\eqref{eq:distance-four-tradeoff}. If $X=2$, then $Y\ge4$. Otherwise $X\ge3$. In this second case, either $Y=1$, forcing $X\ge9$, or $Y\ge2$. Substitution in these three cases proves all three inequalities. The arithmetic deductions have also passed Lean.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Excess forced by a distant singleton]\label{cor:distance-four-excess}
Set $b_j=\min\{6,\alpha_S(x_j)\}$ and $\delta_j=\alpha_S(x_j)-b_j$. Let $J$ be a set of distinct starting positions of occurrences satisfying Proposition~\ref{prop:distance-four}, all read in the fixed order of the snake, with $\alpha_S(x_{r+7})=1$ for every $r\in J$. Then
\[
3|J|\le\sum_j\delta_j,\qquad
G_{12}\ge24\sum_j b_j+72|J|+\sum_{x\in S}\sigma_{12}(x).
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Each occurrence gives $\alpha_S(x_{r+1})\ge9$, hence $\delta_{r+1}\ge3$. Distinct starts have distinct charged positions $r+1$. Summing these inequalities and using nonnegativity of every other excess gives the first assertion. Substitute $T=\sum_j b_j+\sum_j\delta_j$ into $G_{12}=24T+\sum_x\sigma_{12}(x)$ to obtain the second. The local excess implication and the finite-set counting inequality have passed Lean. Combining this correction with another use of the same excess requires a common overlap budget.
\end{proof}


\subsection{Restrictions on the intervening counts}\label{sec:intervening-counts}
The two distant counts do not determine whether the intervening counts can occur together. The next restriction uses three specified positions at a time and the absence of selected cycles.

\begin{proposition}[Intervening-count lower bounds]\label{prop:intervening-counts}
Suppose an induced path has the eight transitions $(a,b,c,d,e,c,a,d)$ starting at $x_r$, where $a,b,c,d,e$ are distinct coordinates. If
\[
\alpha_{r+1}\le3,\qquad \alpha_{r+7}\le3,
\]
then
\[
\alpha_{r+3}\ge5,\qquad \alpha_{r+6}\ge5.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Computer-assisted proof]
Translate $x_r$ to zero and map the five named directions, in order, to $0,1,2,3,4$. Extend this map to a permutation of the nine coordinates. The normalized window has vertex sequence
\[
(0,1,3,7,15,31,27,26,18).
\]
Use the radius-four local model from Section~\ref{sec:radius-four-236}. For the first assertion, impose upper bounds $(3,21,4,21,21,21,3)$ on its seven internal singleton counts. For the second, impose $(3,21,21,21,21,4,3)$. The value 21 places no additional restriction on an internal count. Thus the first model represents a hypothetical violation of the first conclusion under the two stated hypotheses, and the second represents a violation of the second conclusion.

The ordinary encoding excludes coordinate squares and induced six-cycles in three-dimensional faces. Add further finite cycle exclusions, all justified by Lemma~\ref{lem:subset-low-degree}. The first input includes 44 additional cycle exclusions. The second includes four additional translates of one induced ten-cycle, in the four unused coordinate directions. Every retained cycle clause and every degree, endpoint, and count clause follows from the induced-path conditions. The two extracted inputs have 2708 and 1419 clauses, respectively. Their resolution refutations, containing 1141561 and 86400 antecedent references, have been replayed by the sound proof checker. The geometric interpretations and coordinate normalization have also passed Lean checks~\cite{interveningarchive}. Hence both inputs are inconsistent, proving the two implications.
\end{proof}

Acyclicity is essential to this particular local argument. The degree-and-endpoint model for the first input admits a selected set of 142 vertices with counts $(3,5,4,7,12,10,3)$ and three selected cycles. Its saved vertex set has an independent count and degree check. It violates the proposed third-count conclusion, while satisfying the two distant-count caps; its cycles prevent it from being an induced path. Thus degree and endpoint conditions alone cannot establish this local implication.

The joint sum admits a stronger conclusion than adding the two individual lower bounds.

\begin{proposition}[Joint intervening-count bound]\label{prop:joint-intervening}
Under the hypotheses of Proposition~\ref{prop:intervening-counts},
\[
\alpha_{r+3}+\alpha_{r+6}\ge12.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Computer-assisted proof]
Normalize the same eight-transition window. At the third and sixth internal centres, list the 21 eligible opposite corners. Concatenate the two lists, retaining the multiplicity of any repeated vertex. A corner contributes one precisely when it is absent from the selected set. Therefore the number of absent entries in this 42-entry list is exactly the sum of the two singleton counts. This identity follows by counting each list separately and adding; its general version for any finite list of centres has been checked in Lean.

Assume for contradiction that the joint sum is at most 11. Add the corresponding threshold counter to the ordinary local conditions, retaining only the two distant-count upper bounds of three. The counter clauses are justified by their actual input count, using the threshold-counter argument of Proposition~\ref{prop:window-counter}. The joint counter is interpreted separately from the endpoint and individual-count counters. This ensures that its assumed bound is exactly the hypothetical singleton sum.

Every induced path realizing the window would satisfy these clauses and the retained cycle exclusions. The extracted input has 4097 clauses and 1401 variables. A resolution refutation with 5222602 antecedent references was replayed in 174 segments, grouped into 29 kernel checks. The exact preservation of both singleton counts under translation and coordinate permutation transfers the contradiction to arbitrary coordinate labels. The counter interpretation, refutation, and arbitrary-snake deduction have all passed their recorded Lean checks~\cite{jointinterveningarchive}. Thus the assumed upper sum of 11 is impossible.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Joint-count excess contribution]\label{cor:joint-intervening-excess}
Write $b_j=\min\{6,\alpha_j\}$ and $\delta_j=\alpha_j-b_j$. Let $J$ be a set of starting positions, in a fixed orientation of the path, satisfying the hypotheses of Proposition~\ref{prop:joint-intervening} and
\[
b_{r+3}+b_{r+6}\le11.
\]
Then
\[
|J|\le2\sum_{j=0}^{m}\delta_j,
\qquad
G_{12}\ge24\sum_{j=0}^{m}b_j+12|J|+\sum_{x\in S}\sigma_{12}(x).
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
For each $r\in J$, subtract the upper bound on the two truncated counts from the lower bound of 12 on the actual counts. This gives $\delta_{r+3}+\delta_{r+6}\ge1$. Each fixed path position can appear at most once at offset three and at most once at offset six, since both maps $r\mapsto r+3$ and $r\mapsto r+6$ are injective. Summing over $J$ gives the first inequality. Substitute $T=\sum_j b_j+\sum_j\delta_j$ into $G_{12}=24T+\sum_x\sigma_{12}(x)$ and use $24\sum_j\delta_j\ge12|J|$. The excess implication, finite-set summation, and charge arithmetic have also been checked in Lean.
\end{proof}

The excess in this corollary is the same budget used by other count-excess estimates. Adding their bonuses requires a common multiplicity argument. No positive lower bound on $|J|$ has been established. An independent check of the 131 published bank paths finds 1442 oriented occurrences of the direction pattern, but none satisfies both distant-count caps. This observation supplies no frequency theorem.

The further estimate $\sum_{j=2}^{6}\alpha_{r+j}\ge24$ under the same two caps remains numerical. A local forest attains 24, but the exclusion below 24 still requires its own counter interpretation and proof replay. It is not used in the preceding propositions or the global upper bound.

\section{Combining local contributions without repeated counting}\label{sec:common-budget}
Several local theorems may demand excess from the same path position. Their separate charge bonuses cannot be added without checking this overlap. The following result gives one common accounting rule for all seven-centre windows.

\begin{proposition}[Common seven-centre excess budget]\label{prop:common-budget}
Let $J$ be a set of starting indices with $r+8\le m$. For each $r\in J$, let $D_r$ be a nonnegative integer satisfying
\[
D_r\le\sum_{k=1}^{7}\delta_{r+k}.
\]
Then
\[
\sum_{r\in J}D_r\le7\sum_{j=0}^{m}\delta_j.
\]
If several valid lower demands are available for the same window, their maximum may be used as $D_r$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Sum the local inequalities and interchange the finite sums. A fixed path position $j$ occurs only for the seven possible starts $j-1,\ldots,j-7$, and only those belonging to $J$ contribute. Since each $\delta_j$ is nonnegative, the total is at most seven times the full excess sum. If each local demand is at most the same window sum, its maximum is also at most that sum. This proves both assertions. The finite-set summation and maximum rule have passed Lean checks.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Combined count and share charge]\label{cor:combined-budget}
Put $B=\sum_{j=0}^{m}b_j$ and $Q=\sum_{r\in J}D_r$. Let $C$ count a set of distinct forward occurrences of the six-coordinate pattern in Proposition~\ref{prop:three-square-bonus}. Then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:common-budget-charge}
168B+24Q+42C\le7G_{12}.
\end{equation}
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Write $\Delta=\sum_j\delta_j$ and $\Sigma=\sum_{x\in S}\sigma_{12}(x)$. Proposition~\ref{prop:common-budget} gives $Q\le7\Delta$, and Corollary~\ref{cor:occurrence-share} gives $6C\le\Sigma$. Therefore
\[
168B+24Q+42C
\le168(B+\Delta)+7\Sigma
=7G_{12}.
\]
The final integer implication has also passed Lean. The excess and share terms use separate quantities; all excess demands share the same factor-seven budget.
\end{proof}

For example, the joint bound $\alpha_{r+3}+\alpha_{r+6}\ge12$ gives the demand
\[
\max\{0,12-b_{r+3}-b_{r+6}\}
\le\delta_{r+3}+\delta_{r+6}.
\]
The distance-three pair bounds give analogous deficits from nine or ten, and Proposition~\ref{prop:joint-singleton-excess} gives demands eleven or ten. Taking their maximum within a seven-centre window is always justified. Adding them would require a stronger accounting argument.

\subsection{The earlier seven-centre model}
The earlier computational model retains seven normalized directions and six count symbols, giving $34\cdot6^6=1\,586\,304$ states. A transition specifies eight directions and seven counts. It applies the radius-four catalogue to all represented subwindows and adds the identified proved exclusions and demands. The model also enforces Theorem~\ref{thm:outer-pair} in both orientations. Only the four completed cases of the six-centre all-four investigation are used; its remaining unproved cases are admitted. The transition weight is
\[
w=168b_{\mathrm{new}}+24D+42c,
\]
where $D$ is the maximum excess demand on the seven centres and $c\in\{0,1\}$ records one forward share occurrence ending at the new centre. This choice is motivated by~\eqref{eq:common-budget-charge}.

Independent integer verification of the saved graph gives the exact normalized minimum cycle mean
\begin{equation}\label{eq:six-count-mean}
\min_{\gamma}\frac{\sum_{e\in\gamma}w(e)}{168|\gamma|}=\frac{42}{11}.
\end{equation}
The minimum ranges over nonempty directed cycles of this finite graph. Two certificates establish the equality. First, an integer potential $\pi$ of range 8064 satisfies
\[
7056+\pi(v)\le11w(u,v)+\pi(u)
\]
on every one of the $4\,551\,730$ retained transitions. The sharp pair restriction removes 9496 transitions from the preceding graph. Summing around any cycle cancels the potential and proves the lower bound in~\eqref{eq:six-count-mean}. Second, the replacement eleven-step cycle printed in Appendix~\ref{app:replacement-cycle} has appended-count sum 42 and $D=c=0$ at every step. It attains the bound. The arithmetic verification checks every edge of the saved graph; the full graph-generation and arbitrary-snake interpretation have not been checked in Lean.

The cycle proves that these incorporated corrections leave this model's limiting mean unchanged. Its overlapping direction and count records do not specify a globally consistent selected-vertex set, so it supplies no snake construction. The endpoint refinement does not exclude it either: at its occurrence of the pattern in Theorem~\ref{thm:outer-pair}, the relevant counts sum to nine, already exceeding the endpoint-free lower bound eight. Other restrictions are therefore needed even after endpoint information is retained. The result is specific to this finite model and places no limit on stronger local or global methods. The preceding eleven-cycle and its numerical rejection tests remain in Appendix~\ref{app:six-count-cycle}, explicitly distinguished from the replacement cycle.

\subsection{Joint charge and a conditional block estimate}
The count excess and empty-square share can be budgeted together. Put $z_j=24\delta_j+\sigma_{12}(x_j)$, and let $R_i$ be any valid lower bound for $\sum_{j=i+1}^{i+7}z_j$. Distinct seven-centre windows count each position at most seven times. Therefore
\[
\sum_i R_i\le7\left(24\sum_j\delta_j+\Sigma\right).
\]
If several bounds concern the same window charge, their maximum is valid. A count-excess bound $D_i\le\sum\delta_j$ and a share bound $L_i\le\sum\sigma_{12}(x_j)$ on that window give $24D_i+L_i\le\sum z_j$.

\begin{proposition}[Mixed common budget]\label{prop:mixed-common-budget}
Let $B=\sum_jb_j$, $D=\sum_j\delta_j$, and $G=24(B+D)+\Sigma$. Suppose nonnegative demands $Q,C,R$ satisfy
\[
Q\le7D,\qquad6C\le\Sigma,\qquad R\le7(24D+\Sigma).
\]
Then
\[
168B+R\le7G,\qquad
336B+24Q+42C+R\le14G.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The first bound follows by substitution. For the second, use $24Q\le168D$, $42C\le7\Sigma$, and the assumed bound on $R$. The right side becomes $336B+336D+14\Sigma=14G$. The seven-window multiplicity and both algebraic implications have passed Lean. This argument uses separate copies of the same global budget, which explains the factor fourteen.
\end{proof}

The recorded sixteen-step count obstruction of mean $65/16$ illustrates the effect of disjoint shares. Two applicable share-96 windows occupy the cyclic position sets $\{13,14,15,0,1\}$ and $\{6,7,8,9,10\}$. These sets are disjoint. Under their local share premises, a complete period receives at least 192 share units, and its normalized charge is at least
\[
\frac{24\cdot65+192}{24\cdot16}=\frac{73}{16}.
\]
For $p\ge1$ consecutive periods on a line, one crossing window is lost at the boundary. The corresponding conditional share estimate is $\Sigma\ge96(2p-1)$, with the remaining unused boundary shares nonnegative. The finite disjoint-group summation has been checked in Lean.

\begin{proposition}[Conditional finite-block consequence]\label{prop:finite-block-conditional}
Suppose $p\ge1$, $T\ge v+49p+6$, $\Sigma\ge96(2p-1)$, and $24T+\Sigma+840v\le221136$. Then
\[
864v+1368p\le221088.
\]
In particular, these premises are inconsistent with $v=236$ and $p\ge13$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Substituting the two lower bounds in the charge inequality gives
$864v+1368p+48\le221136$. At $v=236$ and $p=13$, its left side without the constant is $221688>221088$. Larger $p$ only increases it. These implications have passed Lean. A full deduction of the stated count and share premises from an arbitrary snake run remains unfinished, so this proposition is not an unconditional exclusion of such runs.
\end{proof}

\section{Longer-window accounting and complete finite models}\label{sec:long-models}
Local share inequalities must be summed with a fixed capacity for every path position. The length of the segment used to prove an inequality and the number of centres charged are separate quantities. Retaining a longer segment can strengthen the local bound while leaving the fivefold share capacity unchanged.

\begin{proposition}[Five-centre accounting with an $n$-transition context]\label{prop:general-window-budget}
Let $m\ge n\ge6$. For every start $0\le r\le m-n$, suppose $L_r$ is a valid nonnegative lower bound on the shares at the final five internal vertices of the segment $(x_r,\ldots,x_{r+n})$. Set
\[
W_n=\sum_{r=0}^{m-n}\bigl(120b_{r+n-1}+L_r\bigr).
\]
Then
\[
W_n+120(n+6)\le5G_{12},\qquad
W_n+4200m\le1100760-120n.
\]
If an integer potential of range $R$ satisfies $p+\pi(v)\le d\,w(u,v)+\pi(u)$ on every transition traced by this sequence, with $p,d>0$, then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:general-window-potential}
(p+4200d)m\le(1100760-120n)d+(n-1)p+R.
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The counted internal indices are $n-1,\ldots,m-1$. The omitted $n-2$ internal vertices contribute at least $n-2$ to $T$, and the two endpoints contribute at least eight. Thus
\[
\sum_{j=n-1}^{m-1}b_j+n+6\le T.
\]
Each share is counted by at most five distinct starts, so $\sum_r L_r\le5\Sigma$. Multiply the preceding count inequality by 120 and add this share inequality to obtain the first assertion. Combining it with $5G_{12}+4200(m+1)\le1105680$ gives the second. Finally, there are $m-n+1$ transitions, so telescoping gives $p(m-n+1)\le dW_n+R$. Substitution yields~\eqref{eq:general-window-potential}. The offset-independent overlap argument and the conditional arithmetic have passed Lean~\cite{geometricarchive}. A particular model still has to establish that every relevant snake traces permitted transitions.
\end{proof}

For $m<n$, the elementary bound $m<n$ suffices whenever the intended upper bound is at least $n-1$. No empty or negative number of windows is used. The earlier eight-transition accounting and its zero-credit obstruction are retained in Appendix~\ref{app:earlier-five-model}.

\subsection{The limit of reweighting a fixed local calculation}
Let $g_1,\ldots,g_5$ be the individual forced-share bounds at five centres, and let $H$ be the larger of their sum and another available total-share bound. For nonnegative coefficients $c,v_1,\ldots,v_5$ with $5c+\sum_i v_i=5$, the weighted share is at least $cH+\sum_i v_i g_i$. This follows by adding $c$ times the total bound and $v_i$ times each individual bound. The six vertices of this coefficient simplex are uniform weighting and weight five on a single centre.

On the saved twenty-transition cycle of the eight-transition model, these six choices have integer means
\[
\frac{2468}{5},\quad\frac{987}{2},\quad493,\quad
\frac{987}{2},\quad\frac{989}{2},\quad\frac{987}{2}.
\]
Their maximum is $989/2$. Linearity therefore bounds every fixed weighting in this relaxation by the same value on that cycle. Even with zero potential-range loss, setting $n=8$ in~\eqref{eq:general-window-potential} requires an integer mean greater than $9400/19>989/2$ to exclude $m=235$. The cycle coefficients were independently checked, and the convexity and boundary implications have passed Lean. This rules out that specific fixed-weight improvement; it does not constrain additional absent-vertex information or stronger count conditions.

\subsection{Complete models before the eleven-transition refinements}
The nine-transition model retains eight normalized directions and five count symbols in each state. A transition supplies nine directions and six counts, at internal positions 3 through 8. Its weight is $120b_{\mathrm{new}}+L$, where $L$ bounds the final five internal shares. Symbol six continues to represent $[6,21]$.

The following are exact minima of the recorded complete finite models. Each value is certified computationally by an integer potential on every transition and a cycle attaining equality. Some inherited exclusions and count-specific shares remain numerical premises, so these computations are separate from the global theorem.
\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{p{0.48\textwidth}r r}
\toprule
Model or added information & Normalized mean & Transitions\\\midrule
Earlier candidate share refinement & $49/12$ & 3042328\\
Eight-transition forced absence & $617/150$ & 3042328\\
Nine-transition memory with old shares & $617/150$ & 11594078\\
Nine-transition forced absence & $3297/800$ & 11594078\\
Eleven-transition count restriction, tracked through history & $18823/4560$ & 11772559\\
Above with eight count-conditioned share patterns & $18823/4560$ & 12893079\\
Above with both further count restrictions & $18823/4560$ & 13173896\\
