\end{center}
The initial candidate refinement has unreplayed share premises. The forced-absence catalogues themselves are proved, while their inherited models retain earlier numerical rules. The complete eight-transition graph has 264384 states, and the nine-transition graph has 1026432. The changes in edge totals after adding history reflect different state spaces; they are not counts of newly allowed geometric configurations.

For the nine-transition forced-absence model, the integer mean is $9891/20$, and the potential range is 12101. Equation~\eqref{eq:general-window-potential} gives the conditional estimate
\[
m\le\frac{22084829}{93891}.
\]
Even replacing this range by zero cannot yield 234, because the required mean would exceed $112680/227$, whereas $9891/20$ is smaller. Additional geometric information is necessary under this accounting.

\subsection{Restricted families and replacement cycles}\label{sec:restricted-family}
The exact potential of the nine-transition graph identifies a minimum-mean family of 49 states and 74 transitions. Extending all its compatible contexts gives a ten-transition graph with 74 states and 121 transitions, and an eleven-transition graph with 118 states and 218 transitions. Twenty-eight extensions are rejected by 27 distinct cycle exclusions. These graphs retain only contexts coming from the selected family; they do not cover arbitrary snakes by themselves.

The progression of exact normalized means in the eleven-transition restricted graph is
\[
\frac{4961}{1200},\quad\frac{2483}{600},\quad
\frac{4973}{1200},\quad\frac{1991}{480},\quad
\frac{9961}{2400}.
\]
These values use, successively, forced absence, shorter vertex splits, eleven-transition vertex splits, complete neighbour choices, and count-conditioned shares. Each restricted potential and attaining cycle has been checked in Lean. Their larger means cannot be substituted for the mean of the complete model, because a path can leave the restricted family.

The first row of Theorem~\ref{thm:three-forced-corners} excludes a transition in this family whose terminal count is two. Every cycle in the saved 118-state graph uses that transition. After its removal, an integer rank increases by at least one on all 217 remaining transitions, and lies between zero and 19. Telescoping the rank proves that every remaining walk has at most 19 transitions; an explicit walk attains 19. The rank table, universal walk implication and attainment have passed Lean. This establishes a limitation on consecutive residence in that family, with no bound on excursions outside it.

The first two share refinements of longer contexts illustrate the same distinction. A particular ten-transition extension of an earlier cycle has normalized mean $413/100$, whereas the complete ten-transition restricted family has the smaller minimum $2477/600$. An attaining cycle is an upper bound on a minimum, not evidence that all cycles have its mean.

\subsection{Exact history and its limitation}\label{sec:history-recognition}
To use an eleven-transition theorem in the complete nine-transition graph, record the relevant prefix of its three successive nine-direction labels. Each label is normalized by first occurrence of its coordinates. A completed prefix triggers the local count restriction or share inequality. The lifted transition still charges the last five internal centres of its nine-transition suffix, so the boundary parameter remains $n=9$.

The first count restriction adds 15552 states, giving 1041984 states in total. All 11162 canonical induced eleven-direction words were enumerated to check that its three-label signature identifies the required word uniquely. The eight unambiguous count-conditioned share patterns and the two further count restrictions yield a final model with 1166400 states. The share rules strengthen 175293 lifted transitions; the three count restrictions exclude 17161 lifted transitions. The complete transition construction and all numerical potential inequalities were independently reconstructed~\cite{newmodelarchive}.

Canonical labels alone do not always identify the longer word. For example,
\[
\begin{split}
&(0,1,2,3,4,2,1,5,6,3,0),\\
&(0,1,2,3,4,2,1,5,6,3,7)
\end{split}
\]
have identical triples of canonical nine-direction labels. Coordinate 0 has left the retained suffix before the last step, so that suffix does not distinguish its return from a new coordinate. The checked share rule for the first word cannot be applied to the second. That rule is omitted from the complete model; recovering it requires more history or another geometric proof. This explicit ambiguity prevents an unjustified identification of local contexts.

All three final models in the comparison table have exact integer mean $18823/38$. Their potentials have range 23459 at denominator 38, and attaining cycles have 38 transitions. The conditional boundary calculation is
\[
(18823+4200\cdot38)m
\le1099680\cdot38+8\cdot18823+23459,
\]
or
\begin{equation}\label{eq:current-conditional-bound}
178423m\le41961883.
\end{equation}
Its integer consequence remains 235. The first restriction improves the complete mean; the extra shares and two later exclusions leave another attaining cycle. No improvement to the global theorem is asserted.

\subsection{The complete remaining minimum-mean family}
For any edge $u\to v$ with weight $w$, define its nonnegative potential slack by
\[
s(u,v)=d\,w+\pi(u)-\pi(v)-p.
\]
Around a directed cycle, the potential terms cancel. If the cycle has mean $p/d$, its total slack is zero. Since every slack is nonnegative, every edge of that cycle is tight. Conversely, every cycle consisting of tight edges has that mean. Thus the tight edges lying in cyclic strongly connected components identify the entire minimum-mean family.

For the final complete model, this extraction gives one component with 70 states and 99 transitions. The two new count exclusions remove the tested windows of a previous attaining cycle, but alternative cycles in this component preserve the same mean. A stronger empty-square estimate must therefore control the whole remaining family and all possible replacements. The extraction is an exact finite computation under the recorded potential. Its complete relation to arbitrary snakes, and the inherited numerical premises, remain separate proof obligations.

\section{Remaining requirements for a stronger bound}\label{sec:closure}
Corollary~\ref{cor:eighteen-empty} gives the immediate target for 234. The longer-window calculations strengthen the available local charge, but the complete model still admits the 70-state minimum-mean family described above. The remaining task has two parts: establish sufficient charge for every permitted sequence, including boundary losses, and discharge every local and graph-coverage premise used in that deduction. A finite rank bound on one restricted family does not control the rest of the model. The following targets specify stronger sufficient global inequalities.

\subsection{Sufficient charge inequalities}
\begin{proposition}[Explicit charge targets]\label{prop:charge-targets}
If every nontrivial snake satisfies $G_{12}\ge114v-191$, then $a(9)\le230$. If the weaker estimate $G_{12}\ge114v-1145$ holds for every nontrivial snake, then $a(9)\le231$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Proposition~\ref{prop:share-loss} gives $G_{12}+840v\le221136$. The first hypothesis gives $954v\le221327<954\cdot232$, hence $v\le231$. The second gives $954v\le222281<954\cdot233$, hence $v\le232$. Subtract one to count edges. The one-vertex case satisfies both conclusions directly.
\end{proof}
These conditional implications have been checked in Lean. Neither hypothesis has been proved for all snakes. A complete improvement requires verified local share bounds, coverage of every allowed sequence, and control of the initial and terminal contributions. Weighted packing is valid under the stated capacities; it supplies no additional credit on the zero-credit cycle of Proposition~\ref{prop:zero-credit}. The targets 230 and 231 remain longer-term conditional estimates.

\subsection{A candidate restriction on consecutive counts of four}\label{sec:four-run}
A direction-independent exclusion of six consecutive counts equal to four would replace several special direction cases by one restriction on the count sequence.
\begin{conjecture}[Six-centre four-count restriction]\label{conj:six-fours}
No snake in $\Q_9$ has six consecutive internal vertices whose singleton-square counts all equal four.
\end{conjecture}
An initial radius-four screen excluded all 132 normalized induced eight-transition patterns with seven internal counts equal to four. A shorter screen then excluded all 34 normalized induced seven-transition patterns with six such counts. The normalization of arbitrary snake segments and the complete 34-case classification have now been checked in Lean. Four numerical exclusions have also passed their proof replays; the remaining numerical cases are pending. Consequently Conjecture~\ref{conj:six-fours} remains unproved. The implication from all 34 exclusions to the conjecture is checked separately.

A five-centre all-four local forest was found and independently checked by explicit vertex and square counting; a separate model also accepted this fixed configuration. Hence the corresponding local-model exclusion cannot be shortened to five centres. This does not establish a complete snake with such a run. An independent scan of the 131 saved 191-edge snakes found maximum run length two. The bank is not exhaustive, so this observation supplies no universal bound. The conjectured six-centre restriction would replace several direction-specific obstructions with one condition on the count sequence.

\begin{proposition}[Conditional frequency consequence]
Assume Conjecture~\ref{conj:six-fours}. Among any $N$ consecutive internal positions, at least $\lfloor N/6\rfloor$ have singleton-square count different from four.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Partition the first $6\lfloor N/6\rfloor$ positions into disjoint blocks of six. Each block contains a count different from four. The sequence argument and its conditional application to arbitrary snakes have been checked in Lean.
\end{proof}

\subsection{A limitation of uniform singleton-density estimates}
The published bank includes a 192-vertex snake for which independent counting gives $T=1632$. This particular census has been checked in Python; its complete connection to the Lean incidence development remains to be checked. It implies that any valid uniform estimate $T\ge cv$ has $c\le17/2$.

The elementary square-census equations alone cannot turn such an estimate into an upper bound below 210 edges. Indeed, the numerical census
\[
(v,a_0,a_1,a_2,a_3)=(211,17,1795,2587,209)
\]
satisfies
\[
a_0+a_1+a_2+a_3=4608,\qquad
 a_1+2a_2+3a_3=36v,\qquad a_3=v-2,
\]
and $a_1\ge(17/2)v$. No path realizing this census is asserted. It demonstrates that these numerical premises permit 211 vertices. The arithmetic implication has been checked in Lean. A stronger conclusion requires additional geometric restrictions, a length-dependent estimate, or another invariant.

\section{A numerical conjecture and its limitations}\label{sec:hypothesis}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{rrrr}
\toprule
$n$ & $a(n)$ & $3\cdot2^{n-3}$ & correction\\
\midrule
3&4&3&1\\4&7&6&1\\5&13&12&1\\
6&26&24&2\\7&50&48&2\\8&98&96&2\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The baseline coefficient is also recovered from successive differences within the two triples:
\[
\frac{7-4}{2^4-2^3}=\frac{13-7}{2^5-2^4}
=\frac{50-26}{2^7-2^6}=\frac{98-50}{2^8-2^7}=\frac38.
\]
Continuing the correction as $1,1,1,2,2,2,4,4,4,\ldots$ gives
\begin{equation}\label{eq:model}
\widehat a(n)=3\cdot2^{n-3}+2^{\lfloor(n-3)/3\rfloor},\qquad n\ge3.
\end{equation}
The terms at dimensions one and two are assigned separately as 1 and 2.

\begin{conjecture}\label{conj:196}
The maximum snake length in $\Q_9$ is $196$ edges.
\end{conjecture}
This numerical conjecture is motivated only by the displayed extrapolation; it is not used in any theorem in this paper. Equation~\eqref{eq:model} predicts $3\cdot64+4=196$.

\begin{proposition}[Model recurrence]
For $n\ge3$, the model satisfies
\[
\widehat a(n+3)+6\cdot2^{\lfloor(n-3)/3\rfloor}=8\widehat a(n).
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Put $t=n-3$ and $c=2^{\lfloor t/3\rfloor}$. Since $\lfloor(t+3)/3\rfloor=\lfloor t/3\rfloor+1$, the left side is
$24\cdot2^t+2c+6c=8(3\cdot2^t+c)$.
\end{proof}
At $n=6$ the recurrence predicts $8\cdot26-12=196$. This is the same evidence as the correction table, not an independent argument.

\begin{proposition}[The known terms do not determine the ninth prediction]
The model
\[
\widetilde a(n)=3\cdot2^{n-3}+1+\left\lfloor\frac{n-3}{3}\right\rfloor
\qquad(n\ge3)
\]
agrees with every known term, after assigning the first two terms separately, and gives $\widetilde a(9)=195\ne196$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
For $n=3,4,5$, both corrections equal 1. For $n=6,7,8$, both equal 2. At $n=9$, the corrections are respectively 3 and 4. Direct substitution proves the claim.
\end{proof}
Only two correction blocks have been observed. No construction currently explains why either proposed correction should describe the actual extremal function.

\section{Construction and the requirements for optimality}\label{sec:optimality}
\subsection{The next-length criterion}
\begin{lemma}[Prefix restriction]\label{lem:prefix}
Every prefix of a snake is a snake.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Restrict the vertex sequence to its initial indices. Distinctness is inherited. Adjacency between any two retained indices has the same value and the same consecutive-index condition as in the original sequence.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Next-length exclusion]\label{thm:next}
For every integer $b\ge0$, all snakes in $\Q_9$ have length at most $b$ if and only if no snake has length $b+1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The upper bound directly excludes $b+1$. Conversely, a snake of any length larger than $b$ has a prefix of length $b+1$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:prefix}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Conjecture~\ref{conj:196} is equivalent to the conjunction of a 196-edge construction and nonexistence of a 197-edge snake. A 196-edge construction would contain a 192-edge prefix and would refute the proposed upper bound 191.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:next} with $b=196$, and restrict a 196-edge snake to its first 193 vertices.
\end{proof}
For the competing optimum 191, the missing exclusion is the finite statement that no sequence $v_0,\ldots,v_{192}\in\{0,1\}^9$ is distinct and satisfies
\[
\Ham(v_i,v_j)=1\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad |i-j|=1.
\]
The pairwise condition is required for every $0\le i<j\le192$. Neither next-length exclusion has been proved.

\subsection{Endpoint maximality and maximum length}
\begin{definition}
A snake $P=(v_0,\ldots,v_L)$ is blocked at its right endpoint when every neighbor $w$ of $v_L$ either equals a vertex $v_i$ with $i<L$, or is adjacent to such a vertex.
\end{definition}
\begin{lemma}[Blocking prevents endpoint extension]\label{lem:block}
A right-blocked snake cannot be extended by appending one vertex while retaining its entire ordered sequence.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
An appended $w$ must be adjacent to $v_L$. If $w=v_i$ for an earlier index, distinctness fails. If $w\sim v_i$ for $i<L$, the appended vertex has a chord to an index at least two positions earlier. Both alternatives contradict the snake definition.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Endpoint maximality does not imply maximum length]
There is a 93-edge snake in $\Q_9$ blocked at both endpoints, although a 191-edge snake exists in the same cube.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The 93-edge sequence in Appendix~\ref{app:witness} specifies 94 vertices. Direct calculation verifies distinctness and the induced adjacency condition for all $\binom{94}{2}=4\,371$ unordered pairs. The appendix gives an earlier blocking vertex for each of the nine endpoint moves, in both the original and reversed orderings. Lemma~\ref{lem:block} excludes extension at either end. Theorem~\ref{thm:lower} supplies a strictly longer snake.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[A sufficient universal blocking condition]
If every 191-edge snake in $\Q_9$ is blocked at its right endpoint, then $a(9)=191$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If a 192-edge snake existed, its 191-edge prefix would admit extension by its remaining final vertex. This contradicts the universal blocking premise. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:next} and the verified lower bound.
\end{proof}
The premise concerns every 191-edge snake. Blocking the 131 recorded classes is insufficient until completeness of the collection is established. Repeated recovery of the same classes is not a completeness proof.

\subsection{Cycles as an alternative construction route}
\begin{theorem}[Deleting a cycle vertex]
Deleting one vertex from an induced cycle of $C$ vertices leaves a snake of length $C-2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Order the cycle as $v_0,\ldots,v_{C-1},v_0$ and delete $v_{C-1}$. Among the remaining vertices, the only surviving cycle edges join consecutive indices. The closing edge involved the deleted vertex; the induced-cycle hypothesis forbids all other edges. The resulting path has $C-1$ vertices and $C-2$ edges.
\end{proof}
\begin{corollary}
An induced cycle with 194 vertices in $\Q_9$ would disprove the upper bound 191.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Deletion gives a 192-edge snake.
\end{proof}
In particular, a 192-vertex coil gives only a 190-edge snake through this deletion operation. Edge and vertex counts cannot be interchanged.

\subsection{The two-layer construction and its obstruction}
Write a vertex of $\Q_9$ as $(v,b)$ with $v\in\Q_8$ and $b\in\{0,1\}$. Vertices in the same layer retain their original adjacency. Across the layers,
\[
(u,0)\sim(v,1)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad u=v.
\]
The same description defines the product of an arbitrary graph with a binary edge. Taylor discusses such lifts in \cite{taylor}.

\begin{theorem}[Exact joining condition]\label{thm:join}
Let $P=(p_0,\ldots,p_r)$ and $R=(q_0,\ldots,q_s)$ be induced paths in a graph. The ordered sequence
\[
(p_0,0),\ldots,(p_r,0),(q_0,1),\ldots,(q_s,1)
\]
is an induced path precisely when
\[
p_i=q_j\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad i=r\ \hbox{and}\ j=0.
\]
When valid, its length is $r+s+1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The layer labels ensure that vertices in different layers are distinct. Each individual path retains its distinctness and induced adjacency conditions. Every cross-layer edge is caused by equality of the base vertices. In the displayed order there is exactly one consecutive pair across layers, at $(p_r,0),(q_0,1)$. Therefore the induced condition requires exactly the stated equality and forbids all other equalities. These conditions also suffice, since all possible pairs have been covered. The vertex count is $(r+1)+(s+1)$, hence the edge count is $r+s+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Single-crossing length constraints]
Assume the known bound $a(8)=98$. A single-crossing snake in $\Q_9$ has length at most 197. Length 196 requires layer lengths $(98,97)$ or $(97,98)$; length 197 requires $(98,98)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The two layer segments are eight-dimensional snakes. Thus $r,s\le98$ and $L=r+s+1$. For $L=196$, $r+s=195$ forces the two possibilities; for $L=197$, both upper bounds must be attained.
\end{proof}
\begin{theorem}[Integer realization of the layer construction]
The map $J(u,b)=u+256b$, for $0\le u<256$ and $b\in\{0,1\}$, is injective and preserves adjacency in both directions between $\Q_8\mathbin{\square}K_2$ and the integer encoding of $\Q_9$. Consequently, compatible layer paths of lengths 98 and 97 give a 196-edge snake under the same definition as the verified witness. Compatible paths of lengths 98 and 98 give a 197-edge snake and would refute the conjectured upper bound 196.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The intervals $[0,255]$ and $[256,511]$ are disjoint, so equality of images forces equality of both components. Flipping a coordinate $k<8$ preserves the layer, because
\[
(u+256)\mathbin{\oplus}2^k=(u\mathbin{\oplus}2^k)+256,
\qquad u\mathbin{\oplus}2^k<256.
\]
Flipping coordinate 8 exchanges $u$ and $u+256$. These are all nine possible flips. Thus adjacency within a layer is exactly eight-dimensional adjacency, while adjacency between layers requires equal base vertices. Compose this map with Theorem~\ref{thm:join}. The lengths become $98+97+1=196$ and $98+98+1=197$. Conversely, every unwanted equality between base vertices creates a nonconsecutive cross-layer edge, so the compatibility condition remains necessary after applying $J$.
\end{proof}
This argument covers snakes that cross a chosen coordinate exactly once. A general snake can cross every coordinate repeatedly. Its intersection with a layer can then be a disjoint union of paths, so bounding that entire intersection by a single-snake length would be invalid.

The explicit compatible paths of lengths 96 and 94 in Appendix~\ref{app:joint-seed} give a 191-edge construction of this form. Restricted tests with a fixed 98-edge seed are described in Appendix~\ref{app:experiments}; their failure does not exclude other seeds or multiple-crossing paths.

\section{Verification scope and remaining problem}\label{sec:verification}
The global conclusion assembled here is $191\le a(9)\le235$. The lower bound follows from the explicit witness. The upper bound uses the square identity, endpoint estimates, all 1287 catalogue keys, a potential verified on every permitted transition, and the path summation. The main argument includes all initial-count and potential losses. No conjecture or restricted construction search enters it.

The completed contributor theorem has a successful archived dependency audit~\cite{bound235archive}. Its assumptions have been discharged in that development; the audit lists propositional extensionality, classical choice, and quotient soundness. The final theorem source and saved audit were reviewed, and the potential was independently reconstructed. A fresh independent rebuild of the entire 235 dependency closure remains pending. The preceding 236 and 238 proof archives are retained separately with their own recorded audits. Appendix~\ref{app:bound236} presents the 236 summation in full; Appendix~\ref{app:bound238} preserves the 238 argument.

The local restrictions and share bounds have the scopes stated in their proofs. Their checked deductions include normalization to arbitrary coordinate labels, semantic interpretation of the finite clauses, and replay of the contradictions. The sharp return witnesses are complete paths. The overlap inequalities, reversal transfer, finite rank certificates, count-saturation deductions and three forced-corner restrictions have separate Lean checks. All cited local dependency audits use only the standard axioms listed above. A local forest supplies only a feasible local assignment; it is not a complete-snake witness.

The successive exploratory graph means are proved as finite statements by an attaining cycle and an all-edge integer-potential check. Some of their count and share rules are still unreplayed solver exclusions, and the complete Lean assembly from arbitrary snakes into these generated graphs remains pending. Their numerical means do not improve the global theorem. Timeouts establish neither existence nor impossibility. The earlier zero-credit cycle and the current 70-state tight family identify different limitations of their respective catalogues. Neither finite family asserts the existence of a corresponding complete snake.

The optimum remains unresolved. No construction above 191 edges has been obtained here. The immediate upper-bound question is whether every hypothetical 235-edge snake has at least eighteen empty squares, or an equivalent sufficiently strong charge. Further work on the selected empty-square route must strengthen shares over the complete 70-state minimum-charge family, control possible replacement cycles, and account for boundary losses. The most recent contributor proposal toward 234 reduces its certificate set and estimated replay cost, but its remaining certificates and graph checks have not been completed~\cite{conditional234archive}. It is excluded from the global theorem here. The conjecture $a(9)=196$ requires both a 196-edge construction and a universal exclusion of 197 edges.

\appendix
\section{The earlier five-window model and its zero-credit obstruction}\label{app:earlier-five-model}\label{sec:current-charge}
For each represented state transition indexed by $i$, take the largest applicable lower bound $L_i$ on the five centres $i+3,\ldots,i+7$, using zero if no recorded rule applies. Bounds on the same window are combined by their maximum. Put
\[
B=\sum_{j=7}^{m-1}b_j,\qquad W=\sum_{i=0}^{m-8}(120b_{i+7}+L_i).
\]
Assume $m\ge7$, with empty sums when $m=7$.

\begin{proposition}[Five-window charge budget]\label{prop:five-budget}
For any valid choice of these share bounds,
\[
W+1680\le5G_{12}.
\]
If a state potential of range at most $R$ satisfies $p+\pi(w)\le d(120b+L)+\pi(u)$ on every transition traced by the snake, where $p,d$ are positive integers, then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:five-potential-bound}
(p+4200d)m\le1099800d+7p+R.
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Each position lies in at most five of the windows, so $\sum_i L_i\le5\Sigma$. The six omitted internal counts and two endpoints give $B+14\le T$. Thus
\[
W+1680=120B+\sum_iL_i+120\cdot14\le120T+5\Sigma=5G_{12}.
\]
Telescoping the potential inequalities gives $p(m-7)\le dW+R$. Equation~\eqref{eq:share-budget-12} and the preceding bound give $W+4200m\le1099800$. Combining yields~\eqref{eq:five-potential-bound}. The overlap, algebraic, and conditional path implications have been checked in Lean; the hypotheses still require a complete graph-to-snake assembly for each generated model.
\end{proof}

The earlier product graph considered in this appendix uses 264384 states, as in the six-centre proof. The following exact finite-model means are established by an attaining cycle and an integer potential checked on every retained transition. The normalized charge is $(120b+L)/120$.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{p{0.60\textwidth}rr}
\toprule
Restrictions and share rules & Mean & Edges\\\midrule
Completed 235 catalogue; counts only & $4$ & 3546419\\
All recorded numerical count exclusions; counts only & $65/16$ & 3042898\\
Same exclusions; first four proved share rows & $57/14$ & 3042898\\
Five further count exclusions and eight numerical share rules, in both orientations & $53/13$ & 3042328\\
Same model; fifth proved share row in both orientations & $102/25$ & 3042328\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The last four rows use ordinary exclusions that have not all been replayed. The last two also retain eight numerical share premises. These are finite-model comparisons under explicit rule catalogues; they do not supply a stronger universal theorem. Even with all their premises accepted, the displayed means and boundary losses give an integer edge estimate of 235. For the last row, the potential has $p=2448$, $d=5$, and range $R=3216$, giving $977m\le229973$.

The fifth share row was selected using the entire preceding minimum-mean component: 57 states and 83 edges. Its pattern labels four edges, and deleting those edges leaves an acyclic graph. An integer rank strictly increases along every remaining edge. Therefore every nonempty closed walk in that component uses the selected pattern. This finite rank argument has been checked in Lean. It explains why one inequality increases the mean from $53/13$ to $102/25$, while leaving open the behavior of replacement cycles.

\begin{proposition}[Zero-credit cycle limitation]\label{prop:zero-credit}
The earlier exploratory graph described in this appendix contains a 25-step cycle whose appended counts sum to 102 and whose recorded share lower bounds all vanish. Any potential inequality using only these recorded share credits has normalized cycle mean at most $102/25$. Selecting or reweighting these zero credits cannot increase the charge on this cycle.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The cycle's appended count sequence is
\[
\begin{gathered}
(6,6,2,5,2,6,3,6,3,2,6,4,3,\\
6,3,4,6,2,3,6,3,6,2,5,2).
\end{gathered}
\]
The saved state transitions close cyclically, each recorded share lower bound is zero, and the counts sum to 102. These finite data are independently checked against the preceding graph archive. Summing $A+\pi_{i+1}\le B b_i+\pi_i$ around the cycle cancels the potentials and gives $25A\le102B$. Every nonnegative weighted sum of the zero credits is zero, including every disjoint packing. Lean checks the displayed sum, general telescoping implication, and zero-credit packing identity~\cite{packingarchive}.

Even with no potential loss, this ceiling cannot exclude 235 through the corresponding single-copy charge accounting. Indeed $25p\le2448d$ implies
\[
(p+840d)235\le219960d+7p+R\qquad(R\ge0).
\]
This arithmetic implication is also checked in Lean. The limitation concerns the lower bounds recorded in this earlier model and finite relaxation. It asserts neither zero actual shares nor realization of the cycle in a complete snake.
\end{proof}

To strengthen that earlier model, a new local inequality must assign positive credit to its surviving family, or a valid additional restriction must exclude its relevant transitions. Increasing constants of rules absent from the cycle, or optimizing their packing, leaves this obstruction unchanged. One sufficient future path target is normalized mean $21/5$ with potential range at most 2111 in the weights $120b+L$. Substituting $p=504$ and $d=1$ into~\eqref{eq:five-potential-bound} gives $4704m\le1105439<4704\cdot235$, hence 234. This is a checked conditional target, without an established universal potential of that strength.


\section{The preceding radius-four bound of 236}\label{app:bound236}
\subsection{The radius-four catalogue and state potential}\label{sec:radius-four-236}
For each prescribed window, use the union of the Hamming balls of radius four around its internal centres. Let $\mathcal F_4$ be the family of 732 normalized direction-and-count pairs in the supplementary catalogue~\cite{radiusfourarchive}. Each pair specifies two to five consecutive internal centres. Count symbols are the truncated values $b_i$, so six represents the interval $[6,21]$.

There are 34 normalized induced direction words of length seven. A state records one such word and the last four internal count symbols. Hence the state set has $34\cdot6^4=44064$ elements. A transition appends one direction and one count, deletes the oldest entries, and renormalizes the directions. It is permitted when the resulting eight-direction word is induced and every new two- through five-centre window avoids $\mathcal F_4$ in both orientations. Every snake determines a sequence of permitted transitions once the catalogue exclusions are proved.

The catalogue and potential table are fixed finite proof data. Their verification has two distinct parts. A hypothetical snake must first satisfy every retained input clause of its local model. A sound contradiction checker then shows that those clauses cannot all hold. The threshold-counter equivalence in Appendix~\ref{app:counters} interprets the auxiliary count variables. For each derived clause, the checker proves that satisfaction of its cited premises implies satisfaction of that clause; reaching the empty clause contradicts satisfaction of the input. A solver's infeasibility report alone is insufficient for this argument.

\begin{lemma}[Radius-four exclusions and potential]\label{lem:radius-four-certificate}
No window of a snake in $\Q_9$ belongs to $\mathcal F_4$. There is a nonnegative integer potential $\Psi$ on the 44064 states just defined, bounded above by 63, such that every induced state transition avoiding $\mathcal F_4$, with appended count symbol $b$, satisfies
\begin{equation}\label{eq:radius-four-step}
42+\Psi(w)\le11b+\Psi(u).
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Computer-assisted proof]
For each of the 732 rows, a hypothetical occurrence is translated and its coordinates are permuted to the canonical window. Restricting the snake to the radius-four neighborhood provides a selected-vertex assignment. The missing-corner identity gives the specified count variables; actual degrees and endpoint indicators give the remaining auxiliary variables. The degree conditions follow from the induced-path definition, and Lemma~\ref{lem:subset-low-degree} justifies each retained cycle exclusion. Thus every retained input clause is satisfied by this assignment. A resolution refutation of that input, replayed through the sound contradiction checker, ends in the empty clause. The resulting contradiction excludes the row. The arbitrary-window reduction, clause implications, all 732 refutations, and their assembly have passed the recorded Lean checks~\cite{radiusfourarchive}.

The potential is specified by an integer at each state. There are 34 normalized induced seven-direction shapes and 132 normalized induced eight-direction shapes. Checking each of the latter against all $6^5$ count choices covers $1\,026\,432$ candidate transitions. Discard nonpermitted transitions and verify~\eqref{eq:radius-four-step} for each remaining transition. The kernel checks this assertion in 34 parts. A separate enumeration of the archived table verifies the same inequality for all 611613 permitted transitions and finds potential values between zero and 48. The bound 63 stated here is the weaker bound used by the Lean summation, and is sufficient. The independent enumeration verifies the potential assertion only; the window exclusions are supplied by the preceding proof certificates.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Radius-four internal count sum]\label{prop:radius-four-sum}
For every $m$-edge snake with $m\ge7$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:radius-four-sum}
42m\le11\sum_{j=1}^{m-1}\alpha_j+291.
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Index the transitions by $d_0,\ldots,d_{m-1}$, where $d_i$ joins $x_i$ to $x_{i+1}$. For $0\le i\le m-7$, let the state at $i$ record $d_i,\ldots,d_{i+6}$ and $b_{i+3},\ldots,b_{i+6}$, and denote its potential by $\Psi_i$. For $i\le m-8$, its next transition appends $b_{i+7}$. The exclusion assertion in Lemma~\ref{lem:radius-four-certificate} makes every state transition along the snake permitted. Sum~\eqref{eq:radius-four-step} over $0\le i\le m-8$ to obtain
\[
42(m-7)+\Psi_{m-7}\le11\sum_{j=7}^{m-1}b_j+\Psi_0.
\]
For $m=7$ the sum is empty and the same assertion holds. Since $\Psi_{m-7}\ge0$, $\Psi_0\le63$, $b_j\le\alpha_j$, and the first six internal counts sum to at least six,
\[
42(m-7)\le11\sum_{j=1}^{m-1}\alpha_j-66+63.
\]
Adding 294 gives~\eqref{eq:radius-four-sum}; in particular, its boundary constant is $294-66+63=291$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Verified upper bound 236]\label{thm:upper236}
Every snake in $\Q_9$ has at most 236 edges. Consequently $191\le a(9)\le236$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $m<7$, the assertion is immediate. Otherwise both endpoint counts are at least four, so Proposition~\ref{prop:radius-four-sum} gives
\[
42m\le11(T-8)+291=11T+203.
\]
The exact square identity and $s_0\ge0$ imply $11T+385(m+1)\le101354$. Combining the two inequalities yields
\[
427m\le101172<427\cdot237=101199.
\]
The integer $m$ is therefore at most 236. The lower bound follows from Theorem~\ref{thm:lower}.
\end{proof}

This earlier proof uses the smaller 732-row catalogue. The six-centre proof in Section~\ref{sec:upper-proof} strengthens its conclusion to 235. The two arguments have separate archived dependency audits. The earlier proof of 238 is retained in Appendix~\ref{app:bound238}.

